export const HOUSE = {
 "writing": "# Adventure Writing\n\nHow a new adventure is written for a LoreMaster, from their answers to a finished adventure in [[ADVENTURE_FORMAT]] (Nate, 2026-10-16). It was worked out by hand on The Stone Warren, and every rule below came from Nate's notes on it. ADVENTURE_FORMAT.md says what a finished adventure looks like. This page says how to get there and what makes one good. STYLE.md holds for every word.\n\n## The questions\n\nThe LoreMaster answers three short rounds. Any question, any round, or the whole set can be rolled instead: the writer answers it from the lore and shows the answer back, to keep, change or roll again.\n\n**Round 1: the table**\n1. Type: Tale, Story, Legacy or Chronicle.\n2. World.\n3. How many Fell, what level, and which lineages if known.\n4. Sessions, and how long each runs.\n\n**Round 2: the story**\n5. The question the adventure answers, in one line.\n6. Who needs the Fell, and why they cannot do it themselves.\n7. Who or what stands against them, and what it wants that makes sense from where it stands. It may be a force.\n8. The count the table measures. Optional for a Tale.\n9. One or two moments the LoreMaster wants to happen.\n10. How it should feel at the close.\n11. What it must touch in canon, and what it must leave alone.\n\n**Round 3: taste and voice**\n12. Fight, talk and explore, as a rough split.\n13. One road with choices, or real forks.\n14. Hard lines for this table.\n15. A paragraph in the LoreMaster's own table voice, about anything.\n\nAfter each round the writer asks up to three follow-ups, only where an answer is thin or clashes with canon.\n\nA rolled answer stays inside canon. It prefers a world with no History yet, gives canon characters a cameo and never resolves them, and never settles a world's Current Challenges in a Tale.\n\n## The stages\n\nEach stage is shown to the LoreMaster, who approves it, changes it, or asks for another take before the next begins.\n\n1. **The spine.** The pitch, the draw-in, the stakes, the conflict, the path to victory, what keeps the players there, why they care, what the LoreMaster gets, the scenes in a line each, and the endings.\n2. **The people and the foes.** Each person's want, secret, voice, and what they do if ignored. How the world meets each lineage at the table. Each foe's rating, want, drive and way past it without a fight. The choices that matter later, with their answers.\n3. **The scene outline.** For each scene: the place and the problem, who is there, the clues and secrets, two or three beats, the checks, and which choices it sets or reads. Every route is listed.\n4. **The writing.** The overview, then each scene, each foe and each character, in ADVENTURE_FORMAT.\n5. **The checks.** A scan against STYLE.md and ADVENTURE_FORMAT.md. Then a cold read by someone who has never run LoreFell, and every finding fixed.\n6. **Notes.** The LoreMaster's notes, in plain words, against a scene, a person or the whole. Only what a note touches is rewritten.\n\n## What makes it good\n\nThese are requirements. Each came from a draft that failed without it.\n\n**The pitch**\n1. The pitch comes first, in two plain sentences a stranger understands. If a new LoreMaster needs a glossary to read it, it fails.\n2. No invented titles or offices (\"mile-warden\", \"circle\"). Use a plain job, such as the scout or the grandmother, or a name. A term from a world page gets a plain gloss the first time.\n3. A people is named by its lineage: the Shrewish, the Vate, the Rekindled.\n4. The story hangs on LoreFell's own words: the Fell, the waking, Discord, Pandemonium, Brands. That is what a new LoreMaster learns first.\n5. The spine says what the players do, what is personal to their Fell, and what the LoreMaster gets: a secret to hold, a voice to play, a reveal.\n6. The opposition may be a person or a force. The spine names the stakes, the conflict, the path to victory, the draw-in, and what keeps the players there to the end.\n7. Vary the engine. Do not write the same theme twice in a row, such as memory, grief or a lost child. Leaning on loss every time is its own tell.\n8. A reroll changes the world, the tone and the engine together, never one line.\n\n**Explaining it to the LoreMaster**\n\n9. Every mechanic, trial, test, riddle and branch is explained to the LoreMaster before it appears. Say what it is, what the outcomes are, and which later scene each outcome shapes. Each scene opens with a \"How this scene works\" reminder.\n10. No codes in the text. A choice that matters later is written in plain words: \"Write down: Furze came, and his food stayed behind.\"\n11. Anything the table tracks (a clock, a count, a supply) has one rule, stated once in the overview. Scenes point to it and never restate it differently.\n12. The overview carries the map of choices: each choice, where it is made, its answers, and what it changes later.\n\n**Foes**\n\n13. Every foe has a want, a reason to be there, and a drive (hunger, fear, orders, a guard's duty, a root that moves it). Every foe has a way past it without a fight. A foe got past by a roll counts as defeated and pays its Lore Points.\n\n**Scenes and the ending**\n\n14. Every scene opens with a read-aloud that says where the Fell are and what just happened. It closes with one that says what it came to and what is ahead.\n15. The night ends on a resolution. The adventure's question is answered in so many words, then what it cost, then a closing read-aloud that ends the session.\n16. Every roll has all four results written, including the rolls inside a fight that win it.\n17. A Brand roll is 1d6 plus the Brand's competency, as CANON.md sets it. A skill never stands in for it.\n18. Every person the Fell might bring along has a rule for the fights: whether foes target them, and whether they can fall.\n\n**Before it is handed over**\n\n19. Before it is handed over, it gets a cold read as a brand-new LoreMaster, and every finding is fixed. If the LoreMaster doesn't understand it, the players won't.",
 "format": "# Adventure Format\n\nHow an adventure is written in `Adventures/`, for every adventure (Nate, 2026-10-15). Adventures here are canon. Each one reads well in Obsidian and turns into ThreadSpire's adventure JSON without hand work.\n\n## The standard\n\nEvery adventure meets all of these.\n\n- **It faces the LoreMaster.** It is written to the person running it, and it holds everything they need to run it start to finish for any table: what is really going on, every scene, every character and foe, every trial and its other ways through, and what to do when the Fell go off the page.\n- **It opens on the waking.** The Fell wake with no knowledge of where they are. The first scene is the waking, and it calls a **Renewal** roll to get up, a **Lore** roll to know where they are, and a **Vigilance** roll to see what is around them, each with a written result for a Fellmark, a Success, a Failure and a Fellstrike.\n- **Every roll is written four ways.** Each skill check names its skill and is rolled as the core mechanic sets it out: 1d6 + the skill against the LoreMaster's 1d6 + Skill Difficulty. Each one gives a Fellmark, a Success, a Failure and a Fellstrike result, following Making the Call. A Fellmark always grows the skill as canon says. A Failure moves the story. A tie is the LoreMaster's call.\n- **A result does not add a penalty.** A Failure or a Fellstrike says what goes wrong in the moment and stops there. No result makes a later roll Unlucky, deals damage, takes something away or adds a foe. The one exception is harm the FellGuide already rules on (a fall, a Trap or Rune that springs): a result that causes it applies the canon rule, links the page, and explains in the result or a reminder beside it how the damage is worked out (Nate, 2026-10-15). Whether a bad roll follows the Fell into the story is the LoreMaster's call, and the adventure's overview says so, with the levers the game gives them.\n- **A Fellmark says the skill increases.** Every Fellmark result ends with *Their (skill) increases by 1.*, so a new LoreMaster knows what to write down.\n- **Skill checks run through the whole adventure**, matched to what the Fell are doing in each scene.\n- **Every result is read to the player.** Each of the four results opens with what the LoreMaster reads to the player who rolled, in the second person (*You see...*), then gives the LoreMaster's note: what changes in play, and what grows.\n- **Read-aloud speaks to the players.** Every read-aloud says *you*, never *the Fell*.\n- **The LoreMaster reads aloud.** Every scene opens with a read-aloud description, and every new place, character and foe gets one. Read-aloud shows and never tells: what the Fell see, hear, smell and touch, and what moves, in short plain sentences. No similes reaching for mood, no \"as if\", no words that name a feeling the table should be left to have.\n- **Each line of dialogue is its own beat.** One speaker per beat. Dialogue sounds like a person talking and fits who is talking: plain words, specific things the speaker would name, people who interrupt themselves and leave things unsaid. No line that ends on a neat closing thought.\n- **Dialogue is full speech.** People talk in whole sentences, run on a little, explain themselves and circle back the way people do. A clipped line of fragments reads as written. Every line must make sense to a table hearing it cold: if a character says \"he\", the table knows who.\n- **New things get a check and an answer.** When a character names something the Fell would not know (the Crimson Haze, a person, a place), a Lore check follows, and that character has a written answer for when the Fell ask about it.\n- **The world reacts to who the Fell are.** For each lineage tied to the adventure's world or its foes, the overview says how the characters and foes react to a Fell of that lineage, and the scenes carry the lines.\n- **Every move gets a read-aloud.** Whenever the scene changes, the Fell move somewhere new, or the place around them changes (after a fight, at a door, at the top of a stair), the LoreMaster reads aloud what is there now, or the Fell make a check (usually Vigilance) to take it in. The Fell never land in the next fight or room without being shown where they are.\n- **Every Fell is accounted for.** When a choice or a roll can split the Fell up (one runs, one hides, one goes another way), the next scene says where each of them is and opens differently for each, with a read-aloud for every place a Fell can be. Never open a scene as if the whole table were standing together unless the adventure has put them there.\n- **The story keeps its facts.** What one scene sets down (who is dead, who is where, what the Fell carry, which room is where) holds in every later scene. When a choice can change a fact, every later scene that touches it has a line for each way it can have gone.\n- **Every route is listed up front.** A scene with more than one way through opens with a reminder that lists every route the Fell can take, what each one costs or gives, and where each leads.\n- **The overview says when and what the world does.** It says what time the adventure runs in and what the world's standing dangers (the Crimson Haze, the cold, the dark) do to the Fell in that time, using canon and adding no new rule. The people in the adventure act on those dangers: nobody stands out in the Haze who would not.\n- **Every split is labeled.** Every branch that applies to only some of the Fell opens with a note to the LoreMaster that begins *This applies to any Fell that...* and names who it is for. A branch for the whole table says so. Each roll inside a branch says which Fell rolls, each result says where that Fell is now, and a scene that ends with the Fell in different places closes by telling the LoreMaster to say where each one is.\n- **Every fight closes the same way.** Name the defeated, the Fatigue check, the Treasure Phase for every Fell who rolled a Fellmark or was granted Luck, then Lore Points. Every Crucible pays through the Treasure Phase. Never write that a foe carries no loot.\n- **The Fell will ask questions.** Each character the Fell can talk to has written answers to the questions a table is likely to ask, each answer its own dialogue beat.\n- **Choices are written out.** Where the Fell choose, the choice lists its options and what each one leads to. At least one choice in every scene that is not a fight.\n- **Trials have other ways through.** Every obstacle gives at least two ways past it, and says what happens if every way fails.\n- **Crucibles name their foes.** Every fight lists its foes in the scene's frontmatter, with how to size it for the table.\n- **Every ending closes on a read-aloud.** Each way the adventure can end, won or lost, has its own read-aloud that closes the night, so the LoreMaster always has words to end the session on.\n- **Foes are written whole.** Every foe page carries the full kit of a Forsaken, whatever its Shatter Rating: its signature Affliction and stance, three Infusions and two Augmentations in the order its ratings unlock them, and three Acts, one at Tier 1, 2 and 3, each a custom build to [[Ability Components]] (one targeting, one damage package, one effect or Affliction, inside its tier's budget, in its archetype's family) that fits the foe as described. The table fields the part its rating allows (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- **Losing goes somewhere.** The adventure says what happens if the Fell lose.\n- **STYLE.md holds** for every word, dialogue included.\n\n## Folders\n\n```\nAdventures/\n  All Adventures.md                 the index\n  <Adventure Name>/\n    <Adventure Name>.md             type: adventure\n    Scenes/NN - <Scene Name>.md     type: scene, in play order\n    Foes/<Foe Name>.md              type: foe\n    Characters/<Name>.md            type: character\n    Locations/<Name>.md             type: location\n```\n\nEvery file name is unique across the vault, so a bare `[[wikilink]]` always resolves. A scene file keeps its two-digit prefix and is linked with an alias, `[[01 - The Waking|The Waking]]`.\n\nA **one-night adventure is scenes only**: its scenes carry `order` and no `act` or `session`, and the adventure's `structure` is `scenes`. A longer adventure sets `structure: acts`, and each scene adds `act` and `session`.\n\n## Frontmatter by type\n\nThe frontmatter is the data, and the body is the telling. A number never appears in both places. Keys are lower case with underscores, lists stay lists even with one item, and links in frontmatter are quoted wikilinks.\n\n**adventure:** `type`, `id`, `title`, `world`, `level`, `fell_min`, `fell_max`, `length`, `structure`, `canon`, `summary`, `synced` (true or false), `adventure_type` (optional: tale, story, legacy or chronicle, the tools' kind of adventure), `scenes` (ordered list of scene links), `characters`, `locations`, `foes`, and while it is checked out to ThreadSpire, `checked_out` and `checked_out_at`.\n\n**scene:** `type`, `id`, `adventure`, `order`, `act` and `session` (acts only, with `act_id` and `session_id` when the tools need the ids kept), `name`, `location`, `crucible` (true or false), `foes` (list of `{foe, count}`), `hazards`, `checks` (list of `{skill, purpose}`), `lore_points`, `teaches`.\n\n**foe:** `type`, `id`, `name`, `shatter_rating`, `build`, `vitality`, `attributes` (all eight), `standard_attack` (`roll`, `base`, `bonus`), `affliction`, `stance`, `infusions`, `augmentations`, `acts` (list of `{name, tier, kind, points, targeting, damage, effect, text}`), `react`, `loot`.\n\n**character:** `type`, `id`, `name`, `role`, `lineage`, `world`, `fate`.\n\n**location:** `type`, `id`, `name`, `world`.\n\nA hazard is `{utility, holds, set_by, springs, spot, clear}`, where `utility` is a canon utility (Trap or Rune) and `holds` links the canon ability or spell inside it.\n\n## The body of a scene\n\nA run of callouts, one per beat, in the order the LoreMaster meets them. The callout type is ThreadSpire's beat kind.\n\n| Callout | Beat kind | Use |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `> [!readaloud] Title` | readaloud | Words the LoreMaster reads to the table |\n| `> [!dialogue] Speaker` | dialogue | One speaker's words. A first line in italics, *If asked who he is:*, says when it is used |\n| `> [!beat] Title` | beat | Something that happens |\n| `> [!choice] Title` | beat | A choice, its options as a list, each with what it leads to |\n| `> [!crucible] Title` | crucible | A fight, with its foes in the frontmatter |\n| `> [!lorecheck] Skill: Title` | lorecheck | A skill check, then its four results |\n| `> [!quest] Title` | quest | A goal the Fell take on. The LoreMaster posts it to the quest board |\n| `> [!clue] Title` | clue | A thing the Fell can find |\n| `> [!secret] Title` | secret | What only the LoreMaster knows |\n| `> [!reminder] Title` | reminder | A rule to keep in mind here |\n\nA skill check is written the same way every time:\n\n```\n> [!lorecheck] Lore: Where am I\n> Who rolls, and what the roll is for, in one line.\n>\n> - **Fellmark.** *Read:* You know this place... *Then:* Their Lore increases by 1.\n> - **Success.** *Read:* You... *Then:* what changes in play, if anything.\n> - **Failure.** *Read:* You... *Then:* ...\n> - **Fellstrike.** *Read:* You... *Then:* ...\n```\n\n*Read* is spoken to the player who rolled, as *you*. *Then* is for the LoreMaster only. A result with nothing to note in play leaves *Then* out.\n\nA scene that branches, such as a choice of roads or a win and a loss, groups each branch's beats under a `## Heading`. A heading becomes a section, shown in the tools as a header between the beats, and the one paragraph right under it (the \"This applies to any Fell that...\" line) is its subheader (Nate, 2026-10-16).\n\nPlain paragraphs between callouts are notes for the LoreMaster and become `general` beats.\n\n## Turning it into JSON\n\nOne adventure becomes one ThreadSpire adventure, written as a `lorefell-adventure-pack-1` pack by the forge's converter. Nothing is typed twice.\n\n- **The adventure** takes `name` from `title` and `desc` from `summary`, and the overview's body becomes its notes. A `scenes` adventure becomes one act with one session.\n- **Each scene** keeps its `id`, and each callout becomes a beat (`kind`, `title`, `body`). A `choice` callout becomes a `beat`. A `## Heading` and a plain paragraph become `general` beats. Wikilinks are written as their display text.\n- **Each dialogue** takes its speaker from the callout title. A speaker who is one of the adventure's characters or foes is linked to that record, so the table shows their portrait.\n- **Each foe** in a scene's frontmatter becomes a library record read from the foe's frontmatter, placed in the scene once. Foes scale to whoever joins, so `count` is the LoreMaster's guide, never a number the table must field. The LoreMaster adds or removes foes at the table.\n- **Each character** becomes an NPC record.\n- **Ids are stable.** Every adventure, scene, beat, foe and character takes its id from the vault, so building the pack again updates the same adventure instead of making a second one.\n\n## Synced adventures\n\nAn adventure whose frontmatter sets `synced: true` lives in the vault, and the vault is its master copy (Nate, 2026-10-15). Its copy in the LoreMaster's account is read-only in its prep: names, scenes, beats and foes change only here. Running it is untouched. Only an admin marks an adventure synced or lifts it. A synced adventure stays private. To publish one, it is unmarked first. An adventure made in ThreadSpire joins the vault when an admin presses Lock to the vault: it is written here in this format under `Adventures/<name>/`, and from then on it is kept here like any other (Nate, 2026-10-15). A change merged to `Adventures/` reaches the LoreMaster's copy within minutes, and the table keeps what it has played.\n\nAn admin may check a synced adventure out to edit it in ThreadSpire for a while: its scenes, beats, foes and their sheets (Nate, 2026-10-15). Checking out adds `checked_out: true` and `checked_out_at` to the overview's frontmatter. While they are there, nothing in the vault changes that folder, Claude included, and the live sync leaves the adventure alone. Save to the vault writes the changes back into the folder and locks it again: a page or a beat nobody touched keeps its own lines, a frontmatter key only the vault reads is kept, and a removed scene, foe or character page is deleted. If the folder changed in the vault since the check-out, the save stops and asks whether to overwrite it or keep the vault's version. Discard changes puts the vault copy back. Either way the mark comes off.\n\nPictures travel as web addresses (Nate, 2026-10-15). An adventure, scene, foe or character page may carry `image:` with the picture's URL, its cover in the tools. A beat's picture is the last line of its callout, `![](https://...)`, which Obsidian shows in place. A picture added in ThreadSpire is written back that way. A page with no `image:` never takes away a picture the table has.",
 "style": "# LoreFell Style Guide\n\nHow all FellGuide content is written and formatted. Claude follows this on every edit. The goal is a professional tabletop rulebook written by a human author, distinct from D&D and Pathfinder, free of AI tells.\n\n---\n\n## Voice and Tone\n\n- Write like a human GM describing the game at the table.\n- Dark fantasy, grounded character drama, weird fantasy. Cosmic horror where it fits.\n- Authoritative without being stiff. Tight pacing. Natural dialogue. Emotionally sharp moments.\n- Show, do not tell. Ground in concrete sensory detail.\n- Prefer unique concepts over derivative fantasy tropes.\n\n---\n\n## Never Use\n\nPunctuation and formatting:\n\n- Em dashes ( or  ) and double hyphens used as em dashes ( -- ).\n- Ellipses for dramatic effect.\n- Semicolons.\n- Italics as emphasis on a single word mid-sentence.\n\nConstructions (AI tells):\n\n- \"X doesn't do this. It does that.\" contrast pairs.\n- \"Not X, but Y\" openings.\n- Triple parallels (\"it burns, binds, and transforms\").\n- Rhetorical questions as section openers.\n- Closing lines that strain for weight (\"carved into the fabric of the Sphere\").\n- Hype filler (\"endless possibilities,\" \"unforgettable,\" \"forge your legend\").\n- Self-describing prose (\"flawed, powerful, and meaningful\").\n- Comparison constructions used as filler.\n\n---\n\n## Editing Rules\n\n- Do not change the author's text unless asked to rewrite it. When only formatting is requested, use the text verbatim.\n- Strip numbered prefixes, image embeds (`![[file.png]]`), and frontmatter blocks from display text. \"3 - Tracking\" becomes \"Tracking.\" \"2.0 - Character Creation\" becomes \"character creation.\" Wiki links follow the Internal Links policy below: keep curated links, strip incidental ones.\n- For cross-file changes, search the whole repo, report scope, then replace in one batch. Never silent sweeps.\n\n---\n\n## Markdown and Formatting Standards\n\n**Callouts** (Obsidian syntax) for mechanic definitions, example skill checks, tips, rules asides, and stat bonuses:\n\n```\n> [!note] Title Here\n>\n> Content here.\n```\n\n**Blockquotes** for opening quotes, closing send-offs, and verse:\n\n```\n> *\"Quote text here.\"*\n>\n> Character Name\n```\n\n- Every skill page carries an `[!note] Example Skill Checks` callout.\n- Skill overview pages end with a two-column table (Skill, Description), not a bullet list.\n- Attribute overview pages end with a three-column table (Attribute, Role, Grants at Creation).\n- Individual attribute pages use a `[!note] Used For` callout.\n- Tables replace plain bullet lists on overview and category pages.\n\n**Lineage pages** get exactly two changes:\n\n1. The bonus line becomes a `[!note] Lineage Bonus` callout with plain, unbolded text inside.\n2. The closing quote becomes a blockquote.\n\n**Origins:** Evasion, Durability, and Resistance origin groups do not grant starting boots.\n\n---\n\n## Internal Links\n\nA link answers a question the reader has where they are: what a term means, where a named thing lives. If a click would not teach the reader something they want at that moment, it is not a link. The failure to avoid is the auto-linker, where every word is a link and none of them mean anything.\n\n- **Once per page, first meaningful mention.** Never link the same target twice on a page. On a long page, once per major section is the most a target gets.\n- **Link terms of art and proper nouns, not common words.** Mechanics, defined concepts, characters, factions, worlds, brands, and artifacts get links. Words like battle, magic, world, and damage never do.\n- **Nothing in headings, callout titles, or flavor quotes.** Links live in body prose and table cells. A link inside a callout body is allowed only when it genuinely helps, and stays rare.\n- **No self-links.** A page never links a term to itself.\n- **Respect the reveal tiers.** A player-facing page never links into the Spoilers section, since the link advertises the secret. Spoiler and lore pages may link among themselves.\n- **Published pages only.** Never link to `_Canon`, `Archive`, or any note excluded from publish. Those render as dead links on the live site.\n- **Alias to keep prose clean.** `[[03 - Tracking|Tracking]]` keeps the file sort prefix while the reader sees \"Tracking.\" Use the same trick for grammar, such as `[[The Veiled Queen|Veiled Queen]]`.\n- **Full paths for ambiguous names.** Where two notes share a basename, write enough of the folder path that the link resolves to the right page. A bare `[[Pandemonium]]` will guess wrong.\n\nStructural links (a landing or overview table linking each row to its page) and definitional links (the first mention of a mechanic linking to its page) are the two kinds that earn their place. Quick Links is a link hub by design and is exempt from the once-per-page rule.\n\nWhen cleaning a page, strip incidental or auto-generated links and any link that breaks a rule above, and keep curated links that follow it. The pre-commit search still flags `[[`, which on a curated page is expected. Confirm each link follows this policy and resolves to a published page rather than stripping it on sight.\n\n---\n\n## Distinct from D&D and Pathfinder\n\nThe fastest way to read as generic is to borrow their nouns. LoreFell has its own vocabulary. Use it, and purge theirs.\n\n| Borrowed term to avoid | LoreFell term |\n| --- | --- |\n| Hit points / HP | Vitality |\n| Armor class / AC | Evasion and Durability resolution |\n| Saving throw, DC, difficulty class | the relevant skill check against the LoreMaster |\n| To-hit roll | the attack's skill roll |\n| Advantage / disadvantage | Lucky roll / Unlucky roll |\n| Spell slots, prepared spells | Spell Attacks and charged abilities |\n| Dungeon Master / DM | LoreMaster |\n| The party / adventurers | the Fell |\n| Experience / XP | Lore Points |\n| Initiative, turn order, your turn | none, combat is simultaneous |\n| Alignment | none |\n\nThe sharpest structural break is the absence of initiative. Combat is simultaneous and cinematic. Never describe combat in turn-based language (\"on your turn,\" \"when your turn comes,\" \"roll initiative\"). Describe rounds, Spotlights, Acts, and Reacts instead.",
 "canon": "# LoreFell Canon Reference\n\nThis is the single source of truth for LoreFell rules and terminology. Before any edit, Claude reads this file and writes to match it. If a vault page contradicts this file, the vault page is wrong and should be corrected, unless we deliberately change the rule here first.\n\nLast verified against the live vault: see CHANGELOG.md for dates.\n\n---\n\n## Core Mechanic (Skyvault Engine)\n\nRoll 1d6 and add your current skill competency, attribute level, or relevant stat. The LoreMaster rolls against you using an opposing skill, a set difficulty, or an enemy stat. Higher result wins. On a tie in a skill roll, the LoreMaster rules the outcome from the story and may call for a reroll. Ties on attacks go to the attacker (see Attack Resolution).\n\n- **Fellmark:** a roll of 6. Critical success. On a skill roll, that skill increases by 1, up to a competency of 5. On an attack, you hit, apply an Affliction, and add a Bonus Damage Roll, 1d6 added to the damage. If that Bonus Damage Roll is also a 6, an Impairment is triggered, and the LoreMaster rolls it on the Impairment table. The LoreMaster may also roll a Fellmark. When both sides roll one, the Fell wins if their skill is higher. If not, the LoreMaster decides.\n- **Fellstrike:** a roll of 1. Critical failure. The action falls apart completely. When both sides roll a Fellstrike, it goes to whoever has the higher skill, Skill Difficulty, or attribute in a combat roll. On a tie, it goes to the attacker, or to the one attempting the skill.\n- **Mastery:** a Fellmark on a skill at competency 5 lets the Fell choose to master it or not. Mastering grants a permanent +1 Mastery, added to every roll with that skill from then on, and resets the skill to competency 0. The first Mastery in a skill unlocks a Talent. A Fell who does not master stays at 5.\n\nSkills begin at competency 0. They do not grow through crystals or Aurum. They grow through use, on Fellmarks. A Motivation is the one other source: when a Fell takes it up, its Attribute rises by 1 and each of its three skills gains 1 point (see The Motivations).\n\nLore Manual is the one sanctioned exception, a rare utility that raises a single skill by 1 competency for Aurum. It exists by design, not oversight.\n\n---\n\n## Attributes and Skills\n\nEight attributes, three skills each, 24 skills total. They fall into three groups: Offensive (Precision, Power, Magic), Defensive (Evasion, Durability, Resistance) and Core (Vigor, Wit).\n\nMobility, Vitality and Luck are Other Attributes. They sit outside the three groups and are not raised with Ascension Crystals. Accuracy, where a page uses it, means your Precision roll.\n\n| Attribute | Skill 1 | Skill 2 | Skill 3 |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Precision | Trickery | Presence | Finesse |\n| Power | Might | Movement | Bearing |\n| Magic | Weaving | Spirit | Creation |\n| Vigor | Endurance | Renewal | Resolve |\n| Wit | Vigilance | Logic | Lore |\n| Evasion | Reflex | Elusion | Poise |\n| Durability | Guard | Temper | Anchor |\n| Resistance | Conviction | Sanctum | Resonance |\n\n### Skill definitions (as written in the vault)\n\n| Skill | Definition |\n| --- | --- |\n| Trickery | Use deception, misdirection, and cunning to achieve your goals. |\n| Presence | Control how others perceive you, whether by drawing attention or avoiding it. |\n| Finesse | Perform precise, delicate, or technically demanding actions. |\n| Might | Apply raw physical strength to overcome obstacles. |\n| Movement | Move your body through the world with speed, agility, and balance. |\n| Bearing | Carry, support, or endure physical burdens. |\n| Weaving | Understand and manipulate magical forces. |\n| Spirit | Connect with souls, emotions, and spiritual forces. |\n| Creation | Shape magic, matter, or energy into something new. |\n| Endurance | Persist through hardship and fatigue. |\n| Renewal | Restore yourself or others after hardship. |\n| Resolve | Remain committed despite fear, doubt, or adversity. |\n| Vigilance | Remain alert to danger, opportunity, and hidden details. |\n| Logic | Analyze information and draw conclusions. |\n| Lore | Recall knowledge, history, and learned information. |\n| Reflex | React instantly to sudden threats. |\n| Elusion | Escape pursuit, capture, or confinement. |\n| Poise | Maintain control, balance, and grace while navigating danger. |\n| Guard | Protect yourself and others from harm. |\n| Temper | Remain standing despite injury or hardship. |\n| Anchor | Remain grounded and unmoved. |\n| Conviction | Protect your thoughts, beliefs, and emotions. |\n| Sanctum | Preserve the integrity of your body and soul. |\n| Resonance | Maintain harmony with magical and supernatural forces. |\n\n### Vigor lane scope (resolved canon)\n\nVigor uses three lanes, not five. The older five-lane set (Resilience, Toughness, Recovery, Tenacity, plus Endurance) is retired. See the retired terms list at the bottom.\n\n- **Endurance:** sustained effort and fatigue. Swimming, marching, labor. Imprint abilities used outside combat.\n- **Renewal:** healing and restoring Vitality, for self or others.\n- **Resolve:** mental afflictions, fear, domination, and supernatural pressure.\n\n---\n\n## Combat Structure\n\nCombat is simultaneous and cinematic. There is no initiative and no turn order. Every combatant commits to their intentions at the start of the round and those intentions unfold together.\n\n### The round (four steps)\n\n1. **LoreMaster Commits.** The LoreMaster secretly records the intended Acts of all enemies. Once recorded, they cannot be changed.\n2. **Players Plan.** Players discuss tactics, coordinate, and decide their approach.\n3. **Everyone Declares.** Each combatant declares their Act and may declare a React. Once declared, Acts are locked.\n4. **Resolve the Round.** The LoreMaster resolves the round through a series of Spotlights, narrated as a cinematic sequence.\n\nEffects, Afflictions, and ongoing conditions activate at the start of the next round.\n\n### Spotlights\n\nA Spotlight is the current focus of the story. The LoreMaster resolves the round scene by scene, choosing which conflict, creature, objective, or event to resolve first based on danger, drama, and importance. All Acts and Reacts tied to a Spotlight resolve before moving to the next.\n\n### Acts and Reacts\n\n- **Act:** the primary thing a character attempts. One per round. Attack, cast, use a skill, use an item, assist an ally, or interact with the environment. Declare what you are trying to accomplish, not just which ability.\n- **React:** a secondary response to something in the round. One per round. May be declared during the declaration step but is not locked there, and can move to a better opportunity. Once spent, it is gone until next round. Changing Armor Stance spends the whole React.\n\nFoes play by the same rules. Every foe, of every Shatter Rating, has one Act and one React each round.\n\n- **A foe's skill roll** is 1d6 + the attribute that governs the skill + the Skill Difficulty.\n- **A foe's breakout** from an Affliction is a Fellmark on the Breakout Skill, as for a Fell. Nothing is added.\n- **A foe's Mobility** is 5, the same as a Fell's. The LoreMaster may raise or lower it for any foe.\n- **A foe's React** draws on everything a Fell's React can do, except Lorebound Aspects and Talents.\n\n### Distance and sight\n\nDistance is measured in a straight line, in spaces, rounded to the nearest whole space. Vision reaches 15 spaces.\n\n### Movement\n\nMovement is a React. Moving spends your entire React and nothing else, and it is never part of an Act. A combatant may move only once per round unless a rule says otherwise.\n\n- **Difficult spaces** (a Mire, rubble, deep snow) cost 2 Mobility each to cross.\n- **Cover:** an attack or spell against a target behind cover is Unlucky. Full cover blocks it.\n\n### Rests\n\nThe LoreMaster calls a rest at any time outside combat, as often as the story allows. A rest that is interrupted gives nothing: no Vitality, no Fatigue shed, no inspections. The rest between sessions is a rest like any other.\n\n### Assisting\n\nAssist another player's skill or attribute roll if you have at least 1 point in that skill or attribute and are adjacent. Add your skill competency or half your attribute level to their total. In battle, assisting costs your React and is declared before their roll. When assisting to remove an Affliction, you roll alongside the ally; if either rolls a Fellmark, the Affliction is removed.\n\n### Charging Abilities\n\nArsenal abilities (weapons, armor, Lorebounds) are tiered and must be charged.\n\n- A basic attack must land to charge Tier 1.\n- A Tier 1 ability must land to charge Tier 2.\n- A Tier 2 ability must land to charge Tier 3.\n- Using a Tier 3 ability successfully removes all charges.\n- A Fell or foe may spend their Act to charge once instead of attacking.\n\nWith no weapon, activating a Skyvault Shard as your Act charges all three tiers at once.\n\nWit is Shard reach. A Fell may pick up a Skyvault Shard from any space within their Wit, and may spend a Shard on any legal target within their Wit, without moving to it. Picking one up inside that range remains a free interaction. Wit still caps how many Shards a Fell can find in a battle at 1 + Wit. Wit also sets how many pieces of equipment a Fell may have equipped at once: 1 + Wit.\n\n---\n\n## Encounter Math (Crucibles)\n\nFour numbers run a fight, and each reads off the party. The LoreMaster sets the party once and the table follows.\n\n- **Average Party Level (APL)** = the levels of every Fell added and divided by their number, rounded to the nearest whole. For a Crucible's foes, each Fell's unspent Ascension Crystals count as levels, so hoarding crystals draws harder foes. Skill Difficulty always uses the party's true levels.\n- **Party Size** = the count of Fell at the table.\n\nFor encounter math, a Fell counts as 7 Vitality for each level they carry. A Fell's own Maximum Vitality is 5 + Vigor at level 1, and each level after adds 1d6 + Vigor (see Leveling).\n\n### Foe Vitality\n\n**Foe Vitality = 7 x Average Party Level x the Shatter Rating's share.** Party size never changes a foe's health. A larger party is answered by fielding more foes, not by thickening the ones already standing.\n\n| Shatter Rating | Share | Worth |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Minion | x0.5 | Half a Fell |\n| Elite | x1 | One Fell |\n| Champion | x1.5 | A Fell and a half |\n| Epic | x2 | Two Fell |\n| Forsaken | x2.5 | Two Fell and a half |\n\nAny foe that fights alone may be padded, whatever its Shatter Rating. Add half a Fell of Vitality (3.5 x Average Party Level) for each Fell past four. Pad only what stands alone.\n\nFoes also carry kit by rating. A foe's level is the Average Party Level plus its rating's offset.\n\n| Shatter Rating | Level offset | Infusions | Augmentations |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Minion | -2 | 0 | 0 |\n| Elite | 0 | 1 | 0 |\n| Champion | +1 | 2 | 1 |\n| Epic | +2 | 3 | 2 |\n| Forsaken | +3 | 3 | 2 |\n\n### Skill Difficulty\n\n**Skill Difficulty = Average Party Level divided by 5, rounded down.** Add it to the value a Fell rolls against.\n\n### Disruptions\n\n**Disruptions = Party Size + Average Party Level.** This is the LoreMaster's pool for each battle, refilled for the next one, spent on both the battlefield and the Foes. The old name Discord Points is retired. Costs are fixed:\n\n| Disruption | Cost |\n| --- | --- |\n| Change Action | 2 |\n| Escape Effect | 3 |\n| Add Equipped Utility | 3 |\n| Fuel a React | 3 |\n| Remove All Afflictions | 5 |\n| Revive an Enemy | 5 |\n| Escalate a Foe | 5 |\n| Environmental Disruption | 8 |\n| Charge All Enemies | 10 |\n| Loose a Discordant again | 12 |\n| Magical Disruption | 15 |\n\nA Foe brought back with Revive an Enemy returns at full Vitality.\n\n### Lore Points\n\n**Lore Points = 1 for each Shatter Rating the party defeated, weighted by that rating.** Count each rating once, no matter how many of that rating stood. At the end of the battle, before Lore Points are given, the LoreMaster declares which foes were defeated. A foe beaten another way, through skills or the story, counts as well as one brought to 0 Vitality. The LoreMaster may also give Lore Points for a session with no Crucible.\n\nAn Ascension Crystal costs the Fell's level + 1 in Lore Points. Lore Points convert into a crystal on their own the moment a Fell has enough. Ascension Crystals may be saved and spent several at once to jump levels.\n\n**Closing a battle**, in this order: the LoreMaster declares which foes were defeated, by any means. Then the Fatigue check, 1d6 + Endurance against the LoreMaster's 1d6 + Skill Difficulty. Then the Treasure Phase. Lore Points are given last.\n\n**Luck stacks** (Nate, 2026-10-14). Each source of Lucky adds one die to the roll, and the roller keeps the highest. Each source of Unlucky adds one die, and the roller keeps the lowest. Lucky and Unlucky cancel one for one, so a roll with as many of each is a normal roll.\n\n| Shatter Rating | Lore Points |\n| --- | --- |\n| Minion | 1 |\n| Elite | 2 |\n| Champion | 3 |\n| Epic | 4 |\n| Forsaken | 5 |\n\n---\n\n## Attack Resolution\n\n### Physical (Power or Precision weapons)\n\n1. Choose a valid target in range and line of sight.\n2. Roll 1d6 + Precision.\n3. Enemy rolls 1d6 + Evasion.\n4. Equal or higher result hits; a tie goes to the attacker.\n5. Damage:\n   - Base Damage: 1 + Power, applied directly to Vitality.\n   - Bonus Damage: weapon's current bonus damage plus stacked effects, reduced by the target's Durability before applying to Vitality.\n\nNote: Power weapons resolve on Precision against Evasion, not Power against Durability.\n\n### Magical Attack (Magic weapon, weapon-style)\n\nSame as a Physical Attack, rolling Precision against Evasion, but Base Damage uses Magic and Bonus Damage is reduced by Resistance instead of Durability.\n\n### Spell Attack (Magic weapon ability)\n\nDoes not roll against Evasion. This covers every spell, Tier 1 to Tier 3, and any skill roll attempted as a spell: it rolls that skill against the LoreMaster. Foes follow the same rule, so a Magic Foe's standard attack is Precision against Evasion and only its spells roll against the LoreMaster.\n\n1. Roll 1d6 + the related Magic skill + any Mastery bonus.\n2. LoreMaster rolls 1d6 + Difficulty.\n3. On success, apply the effect. If it deals damage:\n   - Base Damage: 1 + Magic, applied directly to Vitality.\n   - Bonus Damage: weapon's current bonus damage plus stacked effects, reduced by the target's Resistance before applying to Vitality.\n\n---\n\n## Luck\n\nLoreFell uses a six-sided die for nearly every mechanic. Luck is a visible resource, not hidden.\n\n- The LoreMaster may grant Luck to a Fell for impressive play. Luck counts the same as a Fellmark for the Treasure Phase.\n- **Lucky roll:** roll 2d6 and take the higher result.\n- **Unlucky roll:** roll 2d6 and take the lower result.\n- The LoreMaster may grant a single Lucky Roll to a Fell, a foe, or their own roll.\n- A Fell is eligible for the end-of-battle Treasure Phase only if they rolled a Fellmark, or were granted Luck, since their last rest or the end of their last battle. Fellstrikes do not affect this. A Fell who is not eligible takes nothing from the phase.\n- A Fellmark or a grant of Luck opens one Treasure Phase only. Resting or the end of a battle resets it.\n- A Treasure Roll is never Lucky. The one exception is the Harfolk lineage bonus, an extra lucky die on Treasure Rolls: a Harfolk rolls two d6 and keeps the higher (see The Harfolk).\n- A Fellmark on a Treasure Roll is a Paragon Point and nothing else. A Treasure Roll a Treasure Key lifts to 6 counts as one.\n- Luck is not raised or lowered by Fellmarks or Fellstrikes.\n\n---\n\n## Kit Rulings\n\nNate's rulings, also kept as LoreMaster Ruling notes on each page.\n\n- **Effects** have no Breakout. Most last only until the next round.\n- **Infusions** work only while their weapon is equipped.\n- **Merciless** applies a Fellmark's Affliction twice. A Hexward stops only the first.\n- **Threshold** caps a hit at half current Vitality, rounded up, so it halves a Fell or foe down until a hit can kill.\n- **Bleeding** on a Fell adds their own Base Damage each time they take damage (their best held weapon's, or an unarmed strike's).\n- **Darkshard** opens a 10 space radius. Any overlap silences a foe, and several may stand at once (see Darkshard).\n- **Height and falling:** climbing is a Movement check against the LoreMaster's 1d6 plus the Skill Difficulty, and a success moves half your Mobility in spaces. A fall of 5 spaces or more deals twice the spaces past 4 in Base damage, and each point of Temper ignores a space (see Survival).\n- **Ability building** follows the build rules in Ability Components (components, costs, budgets by tier). Every page and tool follows them.\n- **Undiscovered items** can be carried, equipped and used blind. A Fell knows what kind of thing it is but not what it does. Using one blind is a Logic check against the LoreMaster's 1d6 + Skill Difficulty. Fail it and the item may go wrong, as the LoreMaster decides. A Lore skill check during a rest discovers it (see Survival).\n- **Treasure Key:** a Treasure Roll that ends on 6 is a Paragon Point, including a 5 raised by a Treasure Key.\n- **Harvest Tools** reach up to the highest tier of kit the foe revealed in that fight.\n- **Traders** in any town sell utilities at the price on each utility's page. Facets, Rivets and Shards are never sold. Whip's Emporium carries the rare stock.\n- **Starting points stack.** Lineage, Motivation and Origin each add to skills and attributes. Origin Points place at most 1 in a skill, and when all three point at the same skill or attribute it starts at 3.\n- **Remnant slots** open at levels 10, 20 and 30. A Fell who fills all three is called a god.\n- **Foe stance tiers** are capped by Shatter Rating: Minion none, Elite up to Tier 1, Champion up to Tier 2, Epic and Forsaken all three.\n- **No Damage** is for spells only. A weapon ability always deals damage (Nate, 2026-10-14).\n- **Spell skills** follow the weapon tree: Foci cast on Weaving, Artifact on Spirit, Tome on Creation (FellGuide, Magic Weapons).\n- **Tier 4** exists only on Transcendent Weapons and on Whip's goods. Every other ability or spell is Tier 1 to 3.\n- **Custom abilities and spells** may be attempted at any time through a skill roll. The player rolls Unlucky, and for a higher-impact attempt the LoreMaster may also roll Lucky. A Fell makes a custom ability its own when its weapon levels to unlock a new tiered ability. Abilities on items left by fallen foes can be imprinted.\n- **Lorebounds** cannot be attacked. A Lorebound leaves only when dismissed.\n- **Sundered:** a Fell with no Mastery in any Brand dies and is cast into Pandemonium. A Fell with a Mastery in a Brand is FellScarred instead, and a Brand it has mastered is permanently damaged.\n- **The Pentifax**, the catalogue of foes, is named for the Lost Remnant of summoning.\n- **Tier is set by cost.** The LoreMaster Ability Review prices a player's idea on the Ability Components budget, never by what the effect looks like.\n- **Tier budgets** for abilities and spells: Tier 1 spends 1 to 2, Tier 2 spends 3 to 4, Tier 3 spends 5 or more. Tier 3 has no ceiling. Every page and tool states it this way, never as 5 to 6.\n\n- Faelivra's Talent: The Long Sight shows what the foes committed. Before you and your allies declare, the LoreMaster tells you every Act and React the foes you can see have committed this round. It is the one time the Commit is shown. (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- Cantomyth's Talent: The Pen decides in place of the LoreMaster. You choose the named foe's next Act or React that has not yet resolved. If the LoreMaster already committed it, your choice replaces theirs. (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- Travel through the Skyvault between worlds is rare. Shroomy's Phantom Delights and the Riftrunners are the main way across (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- Hard travel, such as a forced march, may cost a rank of Fatigue on a failed Endurance check. Call it rarely (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- Keeping watch through a rest gives no mechanical benefit (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- The Lore Manual is rare. Traders do not stock it (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n- A Fell who falls on Pandemonium's own ground faces no Aether Strands, and a Skyvault Shard, an unspent Ascension Crystal or Hexpyre still brings them back (Nate, 2026-10-15).\n\n---\n\n## Character Creation\n\n- A Fell's first Ascension Crystal goes into one attribute and sets their starting kit. An Offensive attribute starts them with a Weapon. A Defensive attribute starts them with Armor Stances, and Armor then adds 1 to one more Defensive attribute. A Core attribute starts them with a Lorebound.\n- Each lineage gives the fixed bonus in the Brands table below. The Nyseen alone choose any attribute or skill.\n- There are 18 Lorebounds, and the Unwritten, a custom lorebound a player builds. A Lorebound becomes a Companion at level 4.\n- A Motivation's Title, earned when all three of its skills reach 4 Masteries, raises its Attribute by 3. A Motivation skill at 4 Masteries gains Lucky Rolls on all Lore Checks using it.\n\n---\n\n## Pandemonium\n\nA Fell brought to 0 Vitality falls into Pandemonium. There they face one Aether Strand for each of their levels. Defeated there with an Ascension Crystal, they come back and lose the crystal. Defeated without one, they die for good.\n\n- **An Aether Strand** has Vitality 1, Evasion 3 and no other attribute. Each one standing at the end of a round deals 1 damage. No abilities, no Afflictions. Fellmarks work as usual.\n- **Each round** the fallen Fell takes 1 damage per surviving Strand, then makes a Fatigue check, 1d6 + Endurance against the LoreMaster's 1d6 + Skill Difficulty.\n- **Timing:** the Pandemonium round comes at the end of each round of the fight above, after everyone else has gone. The fallen Fell is cut off from the others. Each fallen Fell faces their own.\n- **The Hexpyre Talent** can call back a Fell Pandemonium has claimed for good, at any time, at the LoreMaster's discretion. The story comes first.\n\nDiscord is in Pandemonium, and every Fell knows it. The Aether Strands a fallen Fell faces there are driven by Discord. This is player-facing because it is a mechanic: every Fell faces it on death (Nate, 2026-10-09). Discord's own lore and past stay Hidden and Forbidden, for LoreMasters only.\n\n---\n\n## Key Terminology\n\n| Term | Meaning |\n| --- | --- |\n| The Fell | The player characters |\n| LoreMaster | The game master |\n| Vitality | A character's health pool (damage applies here) |\n| Fellmark | A roll of 6, critical success |\n| Fellstrike | A roll of 1, critical failure |\n| Lore Points | Experience, convert to Ascension Crystals |\n| Ascension Crystals | Spent to level up and raise attributes |\n| Skyvault Shards | In-combat collectibles with powerful effects |\n| Utility | A thing a Fell carries in the pack. Never called a relic |\n| Ability Item | A Potion, Tablet, Trap or Rune that carries an Act. The LoreMaster gives them in the Treasure Phase, off the foes that fell |\n| Lore Check | Any skill check. A check on the Lore skill is a Lore skill check |\n| Lore Drop | A piece of a Fell's forgotten past the LoreMaster holds back and reveals when the story earns it. How many and when are the LoreMaster's call |\n| Undiscovered | How everything given to a player arrives. A Fell discovers it with a Lore skill check during a rest (see Survival) |\n| Paragon Points | Rare cheat-code currency. A Fellmark on a Treasure Roll is a Paragon Point and nothing else |\n| The Sphere | The multiverse containing all worlds |\n| Stratums | Individual world layers within the Sphere |\n| Discord | The antagonistic force that erases Lore and memory |\n| Lorebounds | Magical familiar companions. 18 in the FellGuide, plus the Unwritten, a custom lorebound |\n| Aspects | Lorebound abilities |\n| Infusions | Passive weapon bonuses, active only while that weapon is equipped |\n| Acts | Primary combat actions |\n| Reacts | Reactive responses in combat |\n| Disruptions | The LoreMaster's pool for each battle (formerly Discord Points) |\n| Halfsworn | A Fell who paid Discord's Toll at awakening, a sliver of them bound to Discord's will. Formerly \"partially Forsaken\" |\n| Forsaken | A Fell who has turned to Discord, one of the Forsaken. The top Shatter Rating takes its name from them, and any foe of that weight may be built at it |\n| Spotlight | The LoreMaster's current focus when resolving a round |\n| Scene | One place and one problem. It ends when either changes. A Crucible is always its own scene. \"Once per scene\" counts by this |\n| Session | One sitting at the table |\n| Act of the adventure | The top level of an adventure: an adventure holds Acts, an Act holds Sessions, a Session holds Scenes. Say \"Act of the adventure\" the first time a page means it, since an Act is also a combat action |\n| Origin Points | Spent at creation to invest in skills |\n| Mastery | Permanent +1 to a skill, chosen on a Fellmark at competency 5. Resets the skill to 0 |\n\n---\n\n## Naming and Voice Conventions\n\n- **Discord** takes no article when it names the antagonistic force. Write \"Discord erases Lore,\" never \"the Discord.\" The article is only correct when it attaches to something else, such as \"the Discord community\" (the chat server) or \"the Discord Wars\" (a named event).\n- **The LoreMaster** is the in-world narrator voice for meta and out-of-fiction pages. Quotes and send-offs on pages like Patch Notes are attributed to The LoreMaster, who owns and tells the story of the Sphere.\n\n---\n\n## Deliberate Design Decisions\n\n- Combat is simultaneous and cinematic. No initiative, no turn order, no phases.\n- Abilities resolve immediately with no tracking.\n- A new ability must not duplicate an existing Affliction, Discordant, or Infusion, and must express its weapon or skill identity even without Affliction text.\n- Target combat length: under 15 minutes.\n- Discordants are a delivery method applied automatically by high-tier enemies, not a separate category.\n\n---\n\n## Cosmology and Hidden Lore (the founding arc)\n\nAuthoritative source pages: `The Lore (Contains Spoilers)/The Histories/The Fallen Lore` and `.../Characters/Canon Characters/Malvaro`. The `Aspects of Discord` page ties this arc to individual worlds. Player-facing pages should hint, never state, anything marked Hidden or Forbidden.\n\n- **Vorce Malvaro** was a Mireleoran scientist who built the Aetherwheel off the heartforge to harmonize the Behemoths (Titans). The Madstone emerged through the Wheel and corrupted his ambition. After his family died and Mireleor turned on him, he opened a rift, took the Ironclad, and became a Stratum-conquering tyrant, branding each world's magic into his flesh.\n- **The Seeker is Malvaro.** \"The Seeker\" is the mythic Mireleoran creator-figure (forger of the Ironclad, raiser of the Primordial Forges) venerated on Mireleor. His aliases include The Seeker, Keeper of Madstone, The Flame Untamed, and Banebreaker. His true identity, and his end as Discord, is Hidden/Forbidden.\n- **Draedon Vesper**, Malvaro's former peer, salvaged the Aetherwheel into a portable engine, rewrote the Ironclad to sever them from Malvaro, and led the Mireleoran survivors into exile. He founded the Fell (the Knights of Fell) to hunt Malvaro across the Sphere.\n- **The Dwellers** are those Mireleoran survivors and their descendants. Player-facing: the most humanlike lineage, \"the adaptable heartbeat of the Sphere,\" found on every world (Valoria, Pyranthia, and beyond) and best known on Valoria. Hidden: they originated on Mireleor and are the creators of the Ironclad. The Dwellers seen on Pyranthia are this same lineage, not a separate people.\n- **Sunderbane** is the Madstone reforged by Draedon into a void-black sword of negation. The final war ran from the Brim (Brimsever) to Valoria, where Draedon drove Sunderbane through Malvaro.\n- **Discord** was born from Malvaro's unraveling in Valoria. Draedon was unmade in the same moment, and Valoria's memory of the event was erased. Discord was later chained in the Vault by the surviving knights.\n- **Aspects of Discord** are Skyvault Shards of Malvaro's torn soul scattered across the Sphere, autonomous fragments that shape their worlds. Named Aspects include the Inkwight (Shadakar), the Crimson Haze (Vyrathis), the Veiled Queen (Valoria), and the Hollow Echo (a drifting fragment of Malvaro's own soul). Several threats written as local dread on the world pages are, at the deepest tier, Discord. The Veiled Queen is the exception among the Aspects, the one shard that did not manifest on its own. It took root in Arandon's widowed queen after grief tore her open, so she alone among the Aspects was once a living person, and her loss of husband and son is the wound that opened Valoria to her. Arandon's death was no lone act. Brannis, pacted with the Veil, drove Sunderbane through Arandon, but the king's own council had already been turned against him from within. It was Vaelric, one of their number, who talked the rest into it, Lady Seraphina among them, and with their consent the king was unmade. The council are all complicit in the murder that shattered the Crown and made the Queen. That body was the Council of Five. Alden the Faithful, sick with his share of the guilt, bound his spirit to the council's old seat at the Sunken Cathedral until the Fell took him, freed him at the Cradle of Embers, and saw him dead. Vaelric fled to the Cradle and became the Veiled Queen's creature, guarding the last shard until the Fell killed him for it. The Fell finished the hunt at the grave of the king, gathered all five shards, and destroyed the Veiled Queen with Sunderbane, the first of the Aspects of Discord to fall. Discord itself endures.\n- **The Silent Cosmology (Forbidden).** Beneath the Malvaro arc sits a deeper structure almost nothing in the Sphere can see. That Discord is in Pandemonium, the realm of the dead, is player-facing (see Pandemonium). What lies under it is not: his consciousness is trapped there. The Scere is Discord's soul, manifest as a world and a cosmic force of judgment, and it is where the Dark Entities are made (not where they dwell). The Silent Realm is a separate entity born at Discord's birth and tied to him only unconsciously, the residue Sunderbane leaves whenever something is erased from a timeline, the place the unmade fall into.\n- **The Dark Entities (Forbidden).** The silent judges, faceless in the same way Discord is faceless, a force of hunger that judges the damned, the souls erased by Sunderbane and the souls devoured by Discord in Pandemonium. They are, in effect, Discord's stomach. They summon the Vate into the realms as judgment given a body (the Vate's \"cosmic force of judgment that never errs\" is them). They carry the LoreMaster's ancient cosmic will, imprinted on them unknowingly, a parental-level influence that gives them motives of their own that no one can read. The LoreMaster does not dare confront them because they are bound to Discord, and Discord is barely aware they exist. Their intention is meant to stay unclear.\n- **The wandering Nyseen soul (Hidden).** Valen (Worriln Below), Emperion (Of Withering Thorns, where he wears a Rysen's body) and Flint (The Light Between Shadows) are one character: a single Nyseen soul, shapebound in the way of its lineage, brought back again and again in different bodies. It has experienced more of the worlds of the Fell than any other soul, rivalled perhaps only by Lantern, Whip or Spindle. Valen was last brought back out of Verdantia, which is why he carries Cyclesong. Player-facing pages hint, never state: all three share one tell, eyes that go black and deep in the right light \"as if they hold a sky no one else can see,\" and a cold, frost-white edge to their power.\n\nReveal tiers:\n- **Player-facing:** Dwellers are a Sphere-wide humanlike lineage; Discord is the force erasing Lore and memory; Discord is in Pandemonium and drives the Aether Strands a fallen Fell faces there; the Aspects exist as world threats.\n- **Hidden:** Dwellers originated on Mireleor as the Ironclad's makers; the Seeker was Malvaro; the named Aspects are fragments of Malvaro; Valen, Emperion and Flint are one Nyseen soul.\n- **Forbidden:** the full Malvaro-to-Discord arc, Sunderbane's origin and purpose, the deepest implications of the Aspects, and the Silent Cosmology (the Scere as Discord's soul, Discord's consciousness trapped in Pandemonium, the Dark Entities as Discord's stomach carrying the LoreMaster's imprinted will, and the Silent Realm as the residue of erasure).\n\n## Brands\n\nA Brand is a world's magic, held like a skill (Nate, 2026-10-13). The full rules are on The Customization/Brands. Each Brand's page under Brands of Magic gives its question, its Example Skill Checks and its Talent.\n\n- Every Fell begins with its home world's Brand at competency 0. A Fell gains a world's Brand at competency 0 when it wakes on that world as the adventure's world. Visiting a world by any other road gives nothing. Brands sit at the bottom of a Fell's Skills.\n- A Fell brought into an adventure from another world keeps only the Brands it holds a Mastery in.\n- **Brand roll:** 1d6 + the Brand's competency + its Mastery, against the LoreMaster's 1d6 + Skill Difficulty. It covers anything the Brand's lore covers, in battle or outside it, as an Act or a React. A Brand roll never deals damage on its own.\n- A Brand grows as any skill does, on Fellmarks, and is mastered the same way.\n- Until its first Mastery, a Brand can be called only on its own world. The first Mastery unlocks the Brand's Talent and lets it be called on any world. A Brand Spindle threads answers on any world. She usually threads a Fell's home Brand, and she can thread any. A Paragon Point traded to or from Spindle is a trade, not a spend, and may change hands outside combat.\n- **Talent:** invoked as an Act or a React. Roll 1d6. A Fellmark: it works at no cost. Any other result: it works and costs one Lore Point, or with none an Ascension Crystal, or with neither a Paragon Point. With none of the three, it fails. A Paragon Point paying for a Talent is a Paragon spend: only in combat, and it burns a Skyvault Shard. Spindle's trade is the one exception to that rule, a trade and not a spend.\n- A Talent lasts one round unless its own words say otherwise. Four last the battle: Spirithral, Grimscour, Rootsummons, and Peltharun until its prey falls.\n- Every Brand a Fell has mastered is a thread Discord can pull. The LoreMaster may call on those threads in the story.\n- A world with no Brand in the master table gives none, unless the LoreMaster grants one where the world's page allows it.\n- **Foes** may roll their own world's Brand as a skill (1d6 + governing attribute + Skill Difficulty). A foe never invokes a Talent.\n- **Summoned and risen allies** (the Spirithral elemental, a Bloodband risen foe) are built as a Minion at the summoner's level, fight on the summoner's side, and are gone when the Talent ends.\n- There are no Wakes and no Severing the Call. Both are retired.\n\n---\n\n## Brands of Magic (master table)\n\nReproduced from Nate's master table. Each world has one lineage, one Brand, and a practice verb, with the lineage's creation bonus alongside. The Strixle Brand is spelled Thyricon (the master table's \"Thryicon\" was a typo).\n\n| Lineage | Bonus | Type | Brand | Verb / practice | World |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Arboril | Creation | Skill | Bramblecraft | Bramblesnare (Snare) | Sylvanoth |\n| Bogle | Presence | Skill | Cantomyth | Mythcasting (Mythcast) | Sable |\n| Boxunin | Durability | Attribute | Bruingar | Bruinbracing (Brace) | Ursathar |\n| Capling | Trickery | Skill | Mycognosis | Mycospreading (Spread) | Sporion |\n| Corvid | Vigilance | Skill | Scrythene | Scrymarking (Scrymark) | Scitnix |\n| Din | Finesse | Skill | Divisyr | Scourging (Scourge) | Natbakka |\n| Dragis | Vitality | Attribute | Sunderlight | Sunderlighting (Light) | Wylv |\n| Dwellers | Resolve | Skill | Ectolume | Tetherghasting (Tetherghast) | Valoria |\n| Elken | Elusion | Skill | Peltharun | Pelttracing (Trace) | Thaloryn |\n| Flayed | Bearing | Skill | Saporset | Saporstilling (Still) | Akkoroka |\n| Foxel | Magic | Attribute | Leyjin | Leybinding (Leybind) | Wildermire |\n| Fribbit | Movement | Skill | Xochitli | Petalooming (Loom) | Mireth |\n| Grimalkin | Mobility | Attribute | Shynderis | Bladestepping (Step) | Felidae |\n| Hallowed | Wit | Attribute | Veriscendence | Veriscending (Veriscend) | Elysara |\n| Harfolk | Luck | Attribute | Echochurn | Luckchurning (Churn) | Burhallow |\n| Harrowed | Conviction | Skill | Grimscour | Grimcarving (Carve) | Brimsever |\n| Ironclad | Endurance | Skill | Aethercraft | Aethershaping (Craft) | Mireleor |\n| Lunarian | Precision | Attribute | Selvyrn | Selvyr (Selvyring) | Sellenia |\n| Marrowkai | Temper | Skill | Bloodband | Bloodbinding (Bind) | Karn |\n| Merkith | Logic | Skill | Tidethane | Tidelinking (Link) | Neriad |\n| Minerite | Power | Attribute | Khaldurgy | Khaldbearing (Khaldbear) | Kwuhara |\n| Nyseen | Choice | Skill or Attribute | Guisewyrd | Guiseshifting (Guise) | Garyx |\n| Rekindled | Renewal | Skill | Hexpyre | Hexkindling (Kindle) | Mortavia |\n| Rysen | Lore | Skill | Luminarch | Luminate | Amaranthia |\n| Shadowkin | Resonance | Skill | Inkthraal | Inkmeld (Inkmelding) | Shadakar |\n| Shrewish | Anchor | Skill | Rootsummons | Rootsummoning (Summon) | Shervinaw |\n| Spireborn | Weaving | Skill | Spirithral | Spirecalling (Call) | Oroniel |\n| Sprittle | Poise | Skill | Cyclesong | Cyclesing (Sing) | Verdantia |\n| Strixle | Evasion | Attribute | Thyricon | Skyrending (Rend) | Avemriol |\n| Sylvakith | Reflex | Skill | Faelivra | Faeliring (Faelir) | Eldarwyn |\n| Thurani | Might | Skill | Tempralis | Tempraling (Tempral) | Therion |\n| Trinumite | Guard | Skill | Chrysalith | Hivestalking (Stalk) | Crixalis |\n| Vate | Sanctum | Skill | Scerinyx | Fatescorn (Scorn) | The Scere |\n| Vyrathe | Spirit | Skill | Hazenry | Ashletting (Ashlet) | Vyrathis |\n| Wolfir | Resistance | Attribute | Gelidane | Frostmending (Mend) | Vulkaris |\n| Wyspar | Vigor | Attribute | Gravenry | Graven | Pandemonium |\n\n## Old Skill Conversion (how former skill names map to current canon)\n\nThe current skills are recorded above under Attributes and Skills and are authoritative. This table records how older skill names convert to the current set, so the conversion is remembered. Apply it in skill-reference context only. Do NOT blind-replace, because most occurrences are ordinary English words in prose (for example \"memory,\" \"balance,\" \"running,\" \"tracking\") or current game terms (Vitality is the health resource, not the old skill; \"elemental damage\" and \"arcane\" are often descriptors, not the skill).\n\n| Old skill | Current skill |\n| --- | --- |\n| Arcane | Weaving |\n| Balance | Poise |\n| Climbing | Movement |\n| Dual Wielding | Finesse |\n| Elemental | Weaving |\n| Inspection | Logic |\n| Jumping | Movement |\n| Lifting | Might |\n| Lockpicking | Finesse |\n| Logic | Logic |\n| Memory | Lore |\n| Mysticism | Spirit |\n| Observation | Vigilance |\n| Recovery | Renewal |\n| Resilience | Sanctum |\n| Running | Movement |\n| Shadows | Spirit |\n| Sneaking | Presence |\n| Speechcraft | Presence |\n| Tenacity | Resolve |\n| Toughness | Temper |\n| Tracking | Vigilance |\n| Trickery | Trickery |\n| Vitality (old skill only) | Endurance |\n\n### Pending conversion in the vault\n\nThese mappings are approved but not yet applied across the vault prose. That is a granular, context-by-context pass, since most occurrences are legitimate current uses rather than old skills. Notably, the Luck page's \"Recovers full Vitality during a Recovery roll\" should become \"Renewal roll\" when the pass reaches it. The earlier link sweep applied Shadows to Presence in three spots; those should be corrected to Spirit during the pass.",
 "crucibles": "# Building Crucibles\n## *what the Fell have to get through*\n\n> *\"Give the thing one reason to be remembered and one way to make the Fell bleed for passing it. The rest is arithmetic.\"*\n>\n> The LoreMaster\n\nA Crucible is a problem with teeth. Most of the time the teeth are Foes and it comes to blows, but the Crucible is the whole scene the Fell have to get through, and sharp Fell might end one without a single strike. The work is deciding what the scene costs them, then handing yourself a card you can run without playing five characters at once.\n\nThere are two ways to put a Foe on the table. Summon one from the Pentifax, the tome of Foes, named for the Lost Remnant of summoning, already shaped and armed. You name it, set its tier, and the table you brought fills in the math. Or forge your own from a handful of choices, the numbers handed to you instead of bought. Both end in the same place, a single Foe Card you read in one glance.\n\n## What every Foe needs\n\nA Foe needs only as much definition as the Crucible will use. You are deciding what it does to the Fell and how hard it is to put down, nothing more.\n\n> [!note] The working parts\n>\n> - **Vitality.** How much punishment it soaks before it drops.\n> - **A build.** Two attributes from the table, set by the scaling. Every other attribute is 0.\n> - **A Standard Attack.** The same basic strike a Fell makes, and the thing that charges its tiers.\n> - **Its Acts.** The tiered abilities it unlocks as it charges, as high as its rating has revealed.\n> - **One Affliction.** Its signature, tied to the Foe itself, landing on its Fellmark.\n> - **One stance.** Shrouded, Stalwart, or Vestments, the way it holds.\n> - **Its loot.** The infusions and augmentations it carries, woven into its nature, taken off the corpse.\n\nA pack of lesser Foes shares one line. A named threat earns its own card.\n\n## The build\n\nA Foe leans on two attributes and nothing else. Every other attribute is 0, which is its weakness as much as the two are its strength. Pick a row or roll your own, and the scaling sets them. One rule holds it together. Give the Foe a way to land, Precision for weapon strikes or Magic for Spell Attacks, or its hits find air unless its Acts carry them.\n\n**Two attacks, no guard.** Glass cannons, all teeth.\n\n| Build | Attributes | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Reaver | Precision, Power | Lands true and hits hard. Stays up only on Vitality and its stance. |\n| Adept | Precision, Magic | Blade and spell, both land clean. Nothing keeps it alive but Vitality. |\n| Scourge | Power, Magic | Overwhelming by hand and by spell, but slow to land a blade with Precision at 0. |\n\n**An attack and a wall.** The workhorses, one lane to threaten and one to survive.\n\n| Build | Attributes | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Stalker | Precision, Evasion | Always lands, never caught. Its blows are light. |\n| Duelist | Precision, Durability | Accurate and grounded, weathers steel. Easy enough to dodge. |\n| Inquisitor | Precision, Resistance | Accurate and warded against magic. A blade still gets through. |\n| Berserker | Power, Evasion | Devastating on a connect and hard to pin. It rarely connects. |\n| Juggernaut | Power, Durability | Slow and unstoppable, soaks everything. Easy to dodge, slow to land. |\n| Golem | Power, Resistance | Strong and spell-proof. Lumbering, and a blade finds it. |\n| Wisp | Magic, Evasion | An elusive spell-thing. It crumbles once cornered. |\n| Idol | Magic, Durability | Casts from behind stone and shrugs off blades. Spells still bite. |\n| Maleficar | Magic, Resistance | A warded sorcerer, hard to out-magic. Soft to steel up close. |\n\n**Two walls, almost no attack.** Spoilers that barely fight.\n\n| Build | Attributes | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Shade | Evasion, Durability | Slips every blow and endures the rest. Gives almost nothing back. |\n| Mirage | Evasion, Resistance | Untouchable by blade or spell. A problem the Fell cannot simply kill. |\n| Bulwark | Durability, Resistance | A wall to steel and to magic. There to hold a line, not to win. |\n\nThose last three are the pacifist that wrecks a fight another way. They block a door, shield the Foe that does the killing, sit on the thing the Fell need, or stall until the world turns. A Mirage that cannot be hit or blasted never has to swing to ruin a plan. When you want a Crucible the Fell cannot punch through, reach here. Epic and Forsaken may take a third attribute as a standout, so the worst threats are not capped at two. Wit and Vigor stay out unless a Foe's concept calls for one, a sage with Lore or a zealot with Resolve.\n\n## The Standard Attack and the Acts\n\nEvery Foe has a Standard Attack, the same strike a Fell makes. Roll 1d6 plus Precision against the target's Evasion, and on a hit deal 1 plus Power as Base Damage with its Bonus Damage on top, reduced by the target's Durability. A Magic Foe makes the same Standard Attack, Precision against Evasion. Only its spells roll Magic against the LoreMaster, its Base Damage 1 plus Magic and its Bonus met by Resistance.\n\nA Foe carries no weapon to assemble, but its strike swings one all the same, leveled like a Fell's. Its Bonus Damage is the weapon bonus read at the Foe's own level, the Average Party Level shifted by its Shatter Rating, the same table a Fell climbs in the Arsenal. A Minion's weapon lags two rungs behind the party and a Forsaken's runs three ahead, the same shift its attributes take. Rating moves the Base through those attributes and the Bonus through the same shift, and opens more of the kit. Infusions that add to Bonus Damage stack on top of it, as they do for a Fell.\n\nThe Standard Attack is always there, and landing it charges the Foe's first tier.\n\nAbove it sit the tiered Acts, built in the ability creator and unlocked by charge, Tier 1 at the first charge, Tier 2 at the second, Tier 3 at the third. Each is built on the same point budget the Fell use, Tier 1 spending 1 to 2 points, Tier 2 spending 3 to 4, Tier 3 spending 5 or more. A Foe climbs only as high as its rating has revealed. The Acts carry the damage and the effects. The Affliction is the Foe's own, handled on its Fellmark.\n\n## The Shatter Ratings\n\nA Foe sits on a ladder from fodder to nightmare, and its Shatter Rating is the whole shape of it in one word. It sets how far above or below the Fell its attributes sit, how much it soaks, how high its Acts climb, and whether the battlefield itself turns when it stands.\n\nThe rating says how many Fell the Foe is worth. A Fell holds 7 Vitality for each level they carry, so a Fell at level 5 holds about 35, and the share below measures the Foe against that.\n\n| Shatter Rating | Attribute offset | Vitality share | Worth | Lore Points |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Minion | -2 | x0.5 | Half a Fell | 1 |\n| Elite | 0 | x1 | One Fell | 2 |\n| Champion | +1 | x1.5 | A Fell and a half | 3 |\n| Epic | +2 | x2 | Two Fell | 4 |\n| Forsaken | +3 | x2.5 | Two Fell and a half | 5 |\n\n| Shatter Rating | Acts | Stance tiers | React | Infusions | Augmentations | Discordant |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Minion | Standard only | none | yes | 0 | 0 | no |\n| Elite | up to Tier 1 | up to Tier 1 | yes | 1 | 0 | no |\n| Champion | up to Tier 2 | up to Tier 2 | yes | 2 | 1 | no |\n| Epic | up to Tier 3 | up to Tier 3 | yes | 3 | 2 | yes |\n| Forsaken | up to Tier 3 | up to Tier 3 | yes | 3 | 2 | yes |\n\nA foe's stance climbs with its charge, as a Fell's does, but only as far as its Shatter Rating allows. Charge past that cap powers its Acts and nothing more in the stance. So a Fellmark always lands on anything below Epic.\n\nEvery Foe, Minion and Elite included, has one Act and one React each round.\n\nThe more of a kind you field, the less each one shows. A Minion brings only its Standard Attack, so it never charges and a swarm of them runs off one shared line. A Forsaken shows everything, and there is only ever one. The work per Foe climbs as the count drops, so the load on you stays flat.\n\nEvery Foe is built to its full Forsaken kit, and the Shatter Rating reveals a slice of it. A Minion shows only its Standard Attack with the rest grayed. Each rung up reveals more, until a Forsaken shows all of it. The kit table is that reveal schedule, not a cap. What is grayed is still there, waiting. Each rung up to Epic adds at least one part, so no escalation is wasted. Epic and Forsaken carry the same kit, and a Forsaken stands apart in its level offset, its Vitality and its Lore Points.\n\nThis is what escalation reaches for. Spending Disruptions to escalate a Foe ungrays its next rung, its Acts, an infusion, an augmentation, the Discordant, and because the kit is already built it can climb past the rating all the way to Forsaken. The body does not change. Its attributes and its Vitality stay where the Crucible set them, so an escalated Champion turns terrifying without turning unkillable. Charge still climbs within whatever has been revealed.\n\nEpic and Forsaken carry a Discordant. When one stands on the field, every Fell takes its signature Affliction the moment the fight begins, before the first blow, no roll and no Fellmark. The Foe's presence is the delivery, and the Affliction it lays is the same one it carries into every strike.\n\n## Scaling to the table\n\nTwo numbers describe the party, and everything else is built from them. The **Average Party Level** is the levels of every Fell added and divided by their number, rounded to the nearest whole. The **Party Size** is the count of Fell at the table.\n\n> [!note] The math\n>\n> - **Build attributes** = Average Party Level + the Shatter Rating's offset, minimum 0, on the two the build names. Every other attribute = 0.\n> - **Vitality** = 7 × Average Party Level × the Shatter Rating's share. No dice.\n> - **Skill Difficulty** = Average Party Level divided by 5, rounded down. Add it to the value a Fell rolls against.\n> - **Ability points** by tier: Tier 1 is 1 to 2, Tier 2 is 3 to 4, Tier 3 is 5 or more. A Foe forges an Act from the same three inlays, the same costs, and the same budgets a Fell uses. Its archetype is its family, and its Form sets the lowest tier it forges. No Damage is for spells only. A weapon ability always deals damage.\n> - **Standard Attack** = 1d6 + Precision against 1d6 + Evasion. On a hit, Base Damage = 1 + Power straight to Vitality, plus Bonus Damage reduced by Durability. A Magic Foe's Standard Attack is also Precision against Evasion. Only its spells roll 1d6 + Magic against the LoreMaster, Base Damage = 1 + Magic, Bonus met by Resistance.\n> - **Bonus Damage** = the Arsenal weapon bonus at the Foe's level, the Average Party Level plus the Shatter Rating's offset: 1 at levels 1 and 2, then 3, 3, 6, 6, 10, 10, 15, 15. Kit bonuses stack on top.\n> - **Lore Points** = the sum of each distinct Shatter Rating defeated, counted once. Ten Minions pay 1. Ten Minions and an Epic pay 1 and 4, which is 5.\n> - **Disruptions** = Party Size + Average Party Level, the pool for each battle, refilled for the next one. Full rules on Disruptions.\n\nThe number of Fell at the table never changes a Foe's health. Seven Fell face the same Elite four Fell face. More Fell are answered by fielding more Foes, not by thickening the ones already standing.\n\n### Padding a Foe that stands alone\n\nAn Epic or a Forsaken alone against a full table of Fell falls fast, because every Fell swings at the same body. There are two answers.\n\nGive it company. An Epic with four Minions is a real fight, and the Minions buy it rounds. This is the first answer and the better one.\n\nOr pad it. Any Foe that fights alone may be padded, whatever its Shatter Rating. Add half a Fell of Vitality (3.5 × Average Party Level) for each Fell past four. Pad only what fights alone. A padded Minion is a slog.\n\nSix Fell at an Average Party Level of 4 face a lone Champion. Its Vitality is 7 × 4 × 1.5 = 42. Half a Fell is 3.5 × 4 = 14, and two Fell stand past four, so it gains 2 × 14 = 28. It takes the field with 70.\n\n### A worked example\n\nSeven Fell at levels 4, 4, 5, 5, 5, 6, 6. They total 35, which divides to an Average Party Level of 5, so a Fell holds 35 Vitality. The LoreMaster sets a Champion and six Minions in a collapsed hall.\n\n| | |\n| --- | --- |\n| Champion Vitality | 53 |\n| Minion Vitality | 18 each |\n| Skill Difficulty | +1 |\n| Disruptions | 12 |\n| Lore Points on victory | 4 |\n\nSix Minions and one Champion pay 1 plus 3, which is 4. The Minions add nothing past the first. Fell are rewarded for what they faced, never for how long the LoreMaster left it standing.\n\nKeep a Foe's two attributes near the Fell's level on purpose. The die is a d6, so once two attributes sit more than about three apart the higher one wins every exchange and the dice stop deciding anything. A Forsaken edges ahead, it does not tower. Its danger is in acting more often, hitting harder when it lands, opening with a Discordant, and refusing to fall. Difficulty and strategy stay in the same place that way.\n\n## Forge your own\n\nBuild the Charnel with me, a Champion for four Fell around level four. Each step is one choice, and the Shatter Rating and the table fill in the rest.\n\n**1. Set the Shatter Rating.** Champion. That hands you the offset of plus one, the Vitality share of one and a half Fell, a React, two infusions, one augmentation, Acts up to Tier 2, and a charge track. You will not spend a point. Its Vitality is the only sum you run, 7 times the average level of four times the share of one and a half, which is 42.\n\n**2. Name it and give it a tell.** Where many died together the dead do not lie quiet. They pull into one body, and a mouth opens in the middle of it that eats its way back through the battle that made it. The longer it feeds, the more of that old fight it becomes.\n\n**3. Choose its build.** Juggernaut, Power and Durability. With the Champion offset of plus one, Power and Durability are each 4 + 1 = 5, every other attribute 0. Read the trade you just made. Its Precision is 0, so it rarely lands a clean blow, and its Evasion is 0, so the Fell strike it at will. It lives anyway, on Durability, its stance, and a deep Vitality pool, and the round its bite does land it takes half a Fell with it.\n\n**4. Set its Standard Attack and its Acts.** The Standard Attack is the same strike a Fell makes. 1d6 plus Precision, which is 0, against the target's Evasion. On a hit its Base Damage of 1 plus Power, 6, lands straight to Vitality, and its Bonus Damage of 6, the weapon bonus at its own level five, the Fell's four raised by the Champion's offset, is met by the target's Durability. Landing it charges the first tier. Above it sit two Acts pulled from the ability creator and built on the component budget.\n\nEvery Act is three inlays, one targeting, one damage package and one effect, and its cost has to fit the tier.\n\nGravemouth is a Tier 1 Act. One Target for 0, Standard Damage for 0, and Crushed for 2, which is 2 points, inside the Tier 1 budget of 1 to 2. The bite closes on one Fell for its 6 Base and 6 Bonus, then closes again, and Crushed deals its 6 Base Damage a second time.\n\nThe Weight of the Dead is a Tier 2 Act. One Target for 0, Double Base Damage for 2, and Bonus Damage for 2, which is 4 points, the top of the Tier 2 budget of 3 to 4. Double Standard Damage costs 3 and would leave no room for an effect, so the weight goes into the Base. The body comes down on one Fell for 12 Base Damage and 6 Bonus with a further 1d6 added to the Bonus, and Powerful means that Bonus ignores 5 of their Durability. It unlocks at the second charge.\n\n**5. Choose its Affliction.** Crippled, tied to the Charnel itself rather than anything it holds. On its Fellmark it eats the strength out of a Fell, every attribute dropping to the lowest. The mouth takes what you were.\n\n**6. Set its stance.** Stalwart. It plants and weathers force, shrugging off damage equal to its Durability as the charge climbs into the stance.\n\n**7. Infuse and augment.** Two infusions and one augmentation, woven into its nature, every one of them loot. Powerful, its blows ignore Durability equal to its Power. Vengeful, strike it and the old wounds answer, the attacker taking its Standard Damage. Emberhold, each blow that would drop it to 0 leaves it standing at 1 instead, with an even chance each time that it dies anyway, because it has already died once.\n\n**8. If it were Epic or Forsaken, its Discordant.** A Champion carries none. Raised to Epic, its Crippled would open the fight laid across the whole table, the Fell feeling their strength go before a blow is struck.\n\nThat is the build. Smaller than a Fell, no Lorebound, no weapon to assemble, the attributes set rather than bought, the rest capped by the Shatter Rating. Here is the card it makes.\n\n> [!note] The Charnel, Champion\n>\n> Where many died together, the dead pulled into one body, and a mouth opened in it that eats its way back through the battle that made it.\n>\n> - **Vitality** 42.\n> - **Juggernaut.** Power 5, Durability 5. Every other attribute 0.\n> - **Standard Attack.** 1d6 plus Precision, which is 0, so it lands only on a high roll. On a hit, 6 Base Damage straight to Vitality and 6 Bonus Damage met by Durability. Landing it charges the first tier.\n> - **Crippled** on its Fellmark. The Fell it catches loses its strength, every attribute dropping to the lowest.\n> - **Stalwart.** As the charge climbs, it shrugs off damage equal to its Durability.\n> - **Powerful.** Its blows ignore Durability equal to its Power.\n> - **Vengeful.** Strike it and the old wounds answer. A Fell who hits it takes its Standard Damage.\n> - **Emberhold.** A blow that would drop it to 0 leaves it at 1 instead, with an even chance each time that it dies anyway.\n>\n> **Acts**\n> - **Gravemouth** (Tier 1, 2 points). One Target, Standard Damage, Crushed. 6 Base and 6 Bonus to one Fell, then its 6 Base again.\n> - **The Weight of the Dead** (Tier 2, 4 points). One Target, Double Base Damage, Bonus Damage. 12 Base Damage and 6 Bonus plus 1d6 to one Fell, ignoring 5 Durability through Powerful.\n>\n> **React**\n> - When a Fell breaks from its reach, it bites once as they go.\n>\n> **Loot:** Powerful, Vengeful, Emberhold.\n\n## Charge, triggers, and the spend\n\nYou run a Foe plain. It takes its Act at whatever tier its charge has reached and lands its Affliction on a Fellmark. It holds its stance. Everything past that comes off one resource.\n\nA Foe charges the way a Fell does, by landing attacks, one integer from 0 to 3 climbing its tiers and deepening its stance. Only Foes with tiered Acts climb by landing attacks. Fodder charges only by spending its Act, as CANON allows any foe, and a swarm stays a single line. This is the same charge the Fell already know, which is the point. Every tool the Fell spend against enemy charge, stances, infusions, augmentations, and triggers has something real to bite. Drained costs it a step, Reapt stops it climbing, Disenchanted strips its infusions, Unclad strips its augmentations. The Foe Card carries its triggers as plain lines with a box to tick the once-a-round ones, so you read the Foe instead of holding it in your head.\n\n> [!note] One pool, two demands\n>\n> The points that turn the battlefield and the points that push a Foe are the same points. Disruptions fund both, so raising a Pale and escalating the boss draw from one reserve, and every spend is a choice against the others. The pool lives on the Disruptions page, sized to the table.\n>\n> Disruptions, the battlefield:\n>\n> | Disruption | What it is | Cost |\n> | --- | --- | --- |\n> | Environmental | fire, flood, collapse, a clinging mire | 8 |\n> | Magical | a curse, a ward, a boon, anything supernatural | 15 |\n>\n> Pales are the most common Disruption and draw from this same pool. Set pieces already in the scene are free. You pay only to conjure mid-fight.\n>\n> Foe pushes, the menace:\n>\n> | Push | What it does | Cost |\n> | --- | --- | --- |\n> | Fuel a React | hand a Foe a React it has already spent | 3 |\n> | Escalate a Foe | reveal its next rung of kit, up to Forsaken | 5 |\n> | Loose a Discordant again | re-lay an Epic or Forsaken's Affliction across the table | 12 |\n> | Change Action | change one Foe's committed Act after the Commit | 2 |\n> | Escape Effect | free one Foe from an Effect on it | 3 |\n> | Add Equipped Utility | give one Foe a utility to use this battle | 3 |\n> | Remove All Afflictions | clear every Affliction from one Foe | 5 |\n> | Revive an Enemy | bring a fallen Foe back into the fight at full Vitality | 5 |\n> | Charge All Enemies | every Foe gains 1 Charge | 10 |\n>\n> All eleven costs are fixed. A React is the cheap tap at 3, an escalation is 5 because it lasts the rest of the fight, and a second Discordant is 12, just under a Magical Disruption, because it floods the whole table at once.\n\nThe pool is spent, not tracked. You set it once at the start of the Crucible and draw it down at the moments that matter. There is no upkeep round to round, no per-Foe state. A Charnel climbs its own charge as it feeds, so most fights you spend nothing on it. Hold your points for the round it reaches its third tier, or the round a Pale would break the Fell open, and decide which one the Crucible needs more.\n\n## Strategy and tactics\n\nThe Crucible is yours to shape, not only to resolve.\n\n- **Aim at the hole.** Every build leaves attributes at 0, and that is where it turns. A Juggernaut is dodged and chipped down. A Maleficar is rushed and cut. Teach the Fell to read the gap and the Crucible becomes a puzzle.\n- **Stack the ratings.** Put a Champion behind a screen of Minions and let the fodder cost the Fell their Reacts before the real threat lands.\n- **Hold the pool.** Spend early and you set the tone fast. Hold it and you break a round open when it counts. With one reserve behind both Pales and escalations, the restraint is the strategy.\n- **Lead with control.** An Epic's Discordant locks Reacts or movement across the table before the first blow, so you open already a step ahead.\n- **Spoil, do not slay.** A Shade or a Mirage does not need to deal a wound. Park it on the objective, the door, or the ally that matters, and the Fell have to solve the room instead of killing their way out.\n- **Let them fight the pool.** Reapt and Drained bite a Foe's charge, and sharp Fell can stall a boss below its full height. Spending Disruptions to force the escalation is how you answer, at the cost of the Pale you wanted to raise instead.\n\n## When it falls\n\nLoot is what the Foe was built with, its full kit and not only the rungs it revealed. The Charnel drops Powerful, Vengeful, and Emberhold, the same parts that punished the Fell while it stood, and its grayed tiers are on the table whether it reached them or not. Only a Fell who landed a Fellmark or earned a grant of Luck since their last rest or the end of their last battle takes any of it, and the payout does not grow with the Foe's Shatter Rating. A Fell imprints one onto their own arsenal, and the thing that nearly killed them becomes the reason they live through the next fight. See the [[Treasure Phase]] for how it lands.\n\n> *\"Everything you bury comes back armed. Build the thing that buried it well.\"*\n>\n> The LoreMaster",
 "makingTheCall": "# Making the Call\n## *the rules end where your judgment begins*\n\nMost of what happens at the table is not in any book. A player describes something and looks at you. The whole game lives in how you answer. This is the everyday version of the work, the calls you make a dozen times a session. The LoreMaster Ability Review covers the larger question of approving a brand-new weapon ability. This covers the rest.\n\n## For the Lore\n\nWhen a Fell reaches for something that would open the story up, let them. This is the rule you lean on hardest. A player who tries the bold, strange, unscripted thing is handing you narrative for free, and your answer is yes wherever you can give it.\n\nSaying yes is not the same as handing them the outcome. Allow the attempt, call Luck fairly, and let the roll feed the world whichever way it lands. The only attempts you turn down are the ones that would stall the table or break canon. An attempt that would grow the story always earns its chance.\n\nCaught between a flat no and a yes that costs something, take the yes. The story is the point.\n\n## When to call for a roll\n\nRoll when the outcome is uncertain and the result matters. That is the whole test. If either half is missing, do not reach for the dice.\n\n> [!note] Do not roll when\n>\n> - The Fell would simply succeed. Let them.\n> - Failure would just stall the story with nothing gained. Find another cost or move on.\n> - The action has no real stake. A locked door the Fell can work at their leisure does not need a roll. Add a guard rounding the corner and the same lock becomes one.\n\nA roll should always be able to go both ways, and both ways should be worth seeing.\n\n## Which skill\n\nThe player describes what they are trying to do. You name the skill that fits how they are doing it. The Skyvault Engine has twenty-four skills for exactly this reason, so the same goal reached two ways calls for two different rolls.\n\nIf more than one skill could fit, let the player make their case and pick the one they argue for. If nothing fits cleanly, fall back to the governing attribute. Never tell a player which skill to use before they describe the action. Let the fiction choose the skill.\n\nThe Fell also hold Brands, and a Brand is rolled \"for anything its lore covers, in battle or outside it, as an Act or a React\" ([[Brands]]).\n\n## How hard\n\nYou are the difficulty, and the difficulty is fixed. When a player rolls 1d6 plus their skill, you roll 1d6 plus the Skill Difficulty.\n\n> [!note] Setting your roll\n>\n> - **Skill Difficulty = Average Party Level divided by 5, rounded down.** It rises by 1 every five levels. At levels 1 to 4 it is 0.\n> - You roll 1d6 + Skill Difficulty against every skill roll. It does not change with the task.\n> - Your lever is Luck. At any time you may make a Fell Lucky or Unlucky, or make your own roll Lucky or Unlucky. A task that should usually cost them is a Lucky roll on your side. A moment they have earned is an Unlucky one.\n> - Luck stacks. Each source of Lucky adds one die to the roll, and you keep the highest. Each source of Unlucky adds one die, and you keep the lowest. Lucky and Unlucky cancel one for one, so a roll with as many of each is a normal roll.\n> - Three things share the word. A Lucky roll is 2d6, keep the higher. The Lucky Effect is a Combat Effect that gives one. A grant of Luck is your reward for impressive play, and it opens the Treasure Phase the way a Fellmark does.\n> - Against a foe, the foe is the difficulty. To make a fight harder, raise a foe's Shatter Rating. See [[Building Crucibles]].\n\nHigher result wins. Call Luck before they roll, not after. The fastest way to lose a table's trust is to move the target once the die is on the felt.\n\n## Reading the result\n\n- **A Fellmark, a 6.** The world notices. The action succeeds and the skill grows by one, up to a competency of 5. A Fellmark on a skill at 5 lets the Fell choose to master it. Give them more than a bare yes. A Fellmark is a moment, so narrate it like one.\n- **Your Fellmark.** You can roll one too. When both sides roll one, the Fell wins if their skill is higher. If not, you decide.\n- **A Fellstrike, a 1.** It falls apart, and badly. A Fellstrike makes the wrong thing happen. The lock breaks in the mechanism. The lie lands on the worst possible ear.\n- **Everything between.** Compare to your roll and narrate the margin. A near miss is a yes with a cost, or a no with a door still open.\n- **A tie.** On a skill roll, you rule the outcome from the story and may call for a reroll. On an attack, the tie goes to the attacker.\n\n## Failing forward\n\nA failed roll should move the story, not freeze it. If a no would stall the whole table, make it a yes that costs something. They get through the door, but the noise brings something. They recall the history, but the memory is wrong in a way they will not learn until later. Keep the world turning even when the Fell stumble.",
 "shaping": "# Shaping an Adventure\n## *Acts, Sessions and Scenes, and what goes in each*\n\nAn adventure in LoreFell has a shape the game already names. Learn the shape once and you can plan anything from one night to a year of play.\n\n## The three levels\n\n[[Running a Session]] sets it out: \"An adventure holds Acts of the adventure, each Act holds Sessions, and each Session holds Scenes.\"\n\n\"Act\" also names the one thing a combatant attempts each round. Say \"Act of the adventure\" whenever the two could be confused, at the table and in your notes.\n\n> [!note] The three levels\n>\n> - **Act of the adventure.** The top level. Canon gives it no fixed size. Use it to gather the sessions that chase one aim, and name it for that aim so the outline shows you the whole adventure at a glance.\n> - **Session.** \"A session is one sitting at the table.\"\n> - **Scene.** \"A scene is one place and one problem, and it ends when either changes. A Crucible is always its own scene.\"\n\nThe scene rule does your planning for you. If a scene in your notes holds two places or two problems, it is two scenes. A fight is always a scene of its own, so the talk before it and the reckoning after it are scenes as well.\n\n## Choose the type first\n\nSettle three things before anything else: the adventure's name, its type and its world. The type tells you how much room the story has.\n\n| Type | Length | What it is |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Tale | One session | A single sitting. One deed or rumor, complete on its own. |\n| Story | 3 to 6 sessions | A short, focused arc for a chosen few Fell. |\n| Legacy | 10 to 12 sessions | The backbone saga. Characters endure and grow. |\n| Chronicle | Open table | A living world many hands shape across the year. |\n\nThose lengths are a guide for planning, and no rule holds you to them. [[The Histories]] records the type of every adventure already played. The Crimson Heir is a Tale, A Court of Ashes is a Story, The Shattering of Valoria is a Legacy, and Echoes Below Burhallow is a Chronicle. Read one of the same type before you write your own.\n\n## The world comes with the waking\n\nThe world you pick is the one the Fell wake on, and waking there is what hands them its magic. [[Brands]]: \"A Fell gains a world's Brand at competency 0 when it wakes on that world as the adventure's world. Visiting a world by any other road gives nothing.\"\n\nSo open on the waking. [[The Story So Far]]: \"Every Fell called since has woken the same way, somewhere strange, with a past in pieces.\" Most of the Histories begin there, and the strong ones put someone who knows the ground close by. In Valoria, Garrik the Old comes through the mist with a lantern. On Vyrathis, Edrath finds the Fell at the stones. That person is the first door into the world, and often the first question about it.\n\n## Give the hunt a count\n\nA long adventure needs a thread the table can measure. The Shattering of Valoria hangs on the five shards of the Crown, and the chapters keep the tally. At the close of chapter 12, \"The Fell held two shards and the Queen held three.\" A clock is made for this: a circle drawn on paper and cut into segments you fill, kept in view of everyone or behind your screen. A public clock tells the Fell how close they are. A hidden one tells you when the ritual completes.\n\n## Let each Act cost something\n\nRaise the price at each Act of the adventure, and take it in the coin only LoreFell has.\n\n- **A mastered Brand.** From the Brands page: \"Every Brand a Fell has mastered is a thread Discord can pull. The LoreMaster may call on those threads in the story.\"\n- **A Paragon Point.** From [[Loot]]: \"Every Paragon you spend pulls on a thread, and threads lead somewhere.\"\n- **A fall.** \"A Fell at 0 Vitality with nothing to save them falls into Pandemonium.\" [[Running Pandemonium]] covers the fight there, and a Fell who comes back has a story to carry.\n\n## Writing it down\n\nKeep the outline on one page: the Acts of the adventure, the Sessions under each, the Scenes under each Session. A scene's notes need little.\n\n> [!note] A scene on paper\n>\n> - **The place and the problem.** One line each.\n> - **Who is there.** The foes and the people of the world, each with a want.\n> - **What can be found.** The Clues and the Secret they lead to.\n> - **Two or three beats.** What you expect to happen, held loosely.\n\nWrite a scene as a short stack of beats in the order you expect them, and expect to use them out of order.\n\n## Preparing a session from the outline\n\nRunning a Session gives the habit: \"Prep the situation, not the outcome.\" Its Light Prep asks for \"Two or three beats you expect to hit, held loosely. The Fell will skip at least one.\" Write those beats into the session's scenes. When the Fell skip one, leave it in the outline. A beat they missed in the first Act can surface in the second.\n\n## A worked example: The Crimson Heir, a Tale\n\n[[The Crimson Heir]] holds the whole shape in one sitting.\n\n**The waking.** The Fell wake at a crossroads on Vyrathis among kneeling statues, and Edrath leads them to his master. Even the waking carries a beat. Malachar summoned them himself, \"which is not a thing the LoreMaster usually permits\".\n\n**The problem.** Malachar states it plainly, and he cannot solve it himself, because \"the oath that had forged the heir would destroy him utterly if he raised a hand against his own blood\". A patron who needs the Fell for a reason he cannot get around is an adventure in one line.\n\n**The scenes.** The Fell split between two places at once, the chapel and the Vault of Breaths, two scenes running side by side. A third, at the Temple of the Still Flame, changes the weapon itself. The climb to Hollowspire and the fight there close the night.\n\n**The cost.** It ends on something that outlives the session. A Keelser rises from the heir's body, \"Plainly no one's ally\", and walks out into a world already forgetting it was saved.\n\n## For any table\n\nWrite the adventure's question in one line before the first scene, and let every Act of the adventure move the Fell closer to the answer. If an Act does not, cut it or fold it into the next.",
 "storySoFar": "---\nreveal: open\n---\n# The Story So Far\n\nThe [[The Sphere|Sphere]] is many worlds layered into one, and it holds together on Lore: the names, histories and truths its people remember. This is how the Fell tell the story of it, as far as anyone knows it.\n\n## The war no one remembers\n\nBefore the worlds you know had names for themselves, a man called Vorce Malvaro went walking through the [[Stratums]] with a hunger that did not end. He broke histories the way other conquerors broke walls. The worlds that fought him crumbled. The worlds that knelt had their cultures and traditions stripped away.\n\nA band of warriors vowed to stop him anyway. They called themselves the Knights of Fell, and they hunted him from world to world until, in the last age of the war, a blade was forged to finish it. Its name was Sunderbane. In the war's final hour, Malvaro fell, and most of the knights' fell with him.\n\n## The hunger that stayed\n\nThe war ended, and something else began. In the ruin it left, [[Discord]] rose. Discord eats Lore. Where it feeds, people forget. A hero's name stops being spoken. A war is fought and lost and wiped away so cleanly that no one alive knows it happened. When enough is gone, the world itself comes apart at the seams.\n\nThe knights who were left paid the last price their order had. They gave themselves to raise the [[The Skyvault|Skyvault]], a seal built from sacrifice, and shut Discord away in [[Pandemonium]], the realm of the dead. It holds, mostly. Not every knight paid the price. The seal has cracked before, and its pieces, the Skyvault Shards, still turn up across the worlds, and never by accident.\n\n## The called\n\nWhen the cracks spread, the [[The LoreMaster|LoreMaster]] began to call. No Fell knows for certain what the LoreMaster is. Some say a power older than the worlds, watching from beyond the Skyvault. Some say only the one at the head of the table, orchestrating the madness. Whatever the truth, when a world begins to slip, the LoreMaster reaches into the Sphere and pulls someone through.\n\nThe first of this cycle was [[The FellGuide/The FellGuide/The Lore (Contains Spoilers)/Characters/Canon Characters/Spindle]], a Lunarian of [[Sellenia]], and she never thanked anyone for it. She treats the call as leverage. She trades in lore, waking the Brands of lineages that have not found them yet in return for what they know, and she fights Discord when and where it suits her.\n\nEvery Fell called since has woken the same way, somewhere strange, with a past in pieces. And every Fell learns the same truth early. Fall in battle and you go down into Pandemonium, where Discord waits, and there you face the Aether Strands, ghostly copies of yourself that Discord drives against you.\n\n## The Shattering of Valoria\n\nCenturies later, four strangers woke in the Whispering Woods of [[Valoria]] with no memory of how they came there. [[Whip]], a Bogle merchant who could talk his way through anything. [[Lantern]], a Hallowed of [[Elysara]] a glowing halo and a dragon's blade. [[Willo]], a Sylvakith who was the heart of the team, and [[Bullsworth]], a Flayed scholar and chef with a bow.\n\nValoria was coming apart. Its king had been erased and his crown broken into five shards, and the ruin's of his kingdom now belonging to the Veiled Queen, an Aspect of Discord who erased anything that looked like the life she had lost. The four hunted the shards across the kingdom, through drowned cathedrals and burning forts, and at the grave of the king they gathered the last of them. There, with Sunderbane, they ended the Veiled Queen. She was the first of the Aspects to fall. The whole tale is [[The Shattering of Valoria]].\n\n## What it cost\n\nWhip kept the blade. Sunderbane will not let its bearer leave a world, so he trades now from [[Shroomy]]'s tavern in a skull, lodged in the seam between worlds, with the blade on his hip. The blade makes him the most hunted thing in the Sphere.\n\nThe others scattered. [[Willo]] left Valoria through a portal at Spindle's side. She had already given up the memories she won back, and bought a Brand from Spindle knowing what it would cost her. [[Bullsworth]] stayed for Serena, and in time he married her. Calevar sat his throne again, and Valoria remembered its king.\n\nLantern paid in a different coin. He had touched the Queen's threads, and when she fell they dragged him up into Pandemonium, where Discord waited. [[Glower]], the First Riftrunner, pulled him out, and Lantern took up the Riftrunners' work, sealing the ruptures between worlds and running down Discord's agents.\n\nThe past found him in Elysara. Long before he was ever Fell, Lantern had cast a Hallowed named Magnus into the Brim. Magnus came back called like any other Fell, with no memory of it. In [[Mireleor]], deep in the sealed city of Veyrun-Zar, he died, and in Pandemonium Discord gave him back everything he had lost, the Brim and the betrayal with it, and offered him vengeance. He took it. At the bottom of that world the forge chose him to carry its chained sun, and he walked into it and was gone. The whole tale is [[Madness of Mireleor]].\n\nHe came out of it as [[Magnus Bane]], Discord's hand, the Blazing Sun at his side, hunting the summoning tome called the Pentifax. When they met in Elysara, Lantern stood against him and lost. Magnus took his head and burned the rest.\n\nGlower carried the skull away. In the ruins of [[Mireleor]] he dug out old Ironclad workings and built Lantern a new body around the last of his soul. Most of him came back. Some of him sits at a distance now, and some memories repeat. He hunts Discord's agents still.\n\n## Other Fell, other worlds\n\nValoria was one world of many. The LoreMaster kept calling.\n\nBefore Valoria, in the glass wastes of [[Pyranthia]], [[Shroomy]] woke beside Kreven and Rysn. Zarathis meant to finish his ascension and fold the Stratums into one, and the three of them stopped him. Rysn sundered him with a thrown fork. They took his astral pocket for their own, and Shroomy came out of it one of the strongest Fell in the Sphere. She keeps a tavern in a skull between the worlds, and her restaurant, Shroomy's Phantom Delights, floats on an asteroid of dead god bones beyond the Skyvault.\n\nIn the warrens of [[Burhallow]], [[Pippa]] and [[Acantha]] went down after the Silent Choir. Acantha died twice getting there and fought ten copies of herself in Pandemonium. They came up as primarchs of Maelor, bound to the FateWell. In [[Mireth]], [[Emperion]] found what the Council had buried, threw it onto a wall for the whole city to see, and stayed. On [[Karn]], [[Selvara]] fought her way into the Bonecaller's citadel, pressed a Brand against a Paragon at its heart, and Vaegra and Karn's Brand was gone. In [[Vyrathis]], a blade from the line of Sunderbane ended the Crimson Heir.\n\n## The burning of Shadakar\n\nOn [[Shadakar]], six strangers woke in the hold of a prison ship and went hunting the Keyshards of the Nadir Dial before the eclipse waned. [[Tamesis]] sailed with them and [[Astra]]. Tamesis made bargains of her own. In Pandemonium she called Discord sire, and Discord told her what it wanted: Spindle, found and branded.\n\nWhen the Dial turned, the Magnus came down out of the eclipse with a broken halo, a head in one hand and a burning book in the other, and the Hallowed came with him. The Abysm burned. The Inkwight, one of the Aspects of Discord, burned with it, and Discord came away a little more whole. The crew ran for [[Elysara]] and found it already half burned. On Shadakar they say Magnus Bane offered its people a bargain there, and Shadakar paid for it.\n\n## Now\n\nThat is where the story stands. Discord presses on every edge of every world, and it is looking for Spindle. The Skyvault holds, mostly. Somewhere a world is beginning to slip, and the LoreMaster is reaching into the Sphere again, for you."
};

